window.QUESTION_BANK = [{"id":1,"question":"Which of the following best describes Word2Vec?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A programming language used to build artificial intelligence models."},{"key":"b","text":"A statistical technique used to analyze word frequency in a text corpus."},{"key":"c","text":"A deep learning algorithm used to generate word embeddings from text data."},{"key":"d","text":"A database management system designed for storing and querying word data."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Word2Vec learns a dense vector for each word from the words that tend to appear around it. Words used in similar contexts acquire vectors that are close in the embedding space. It is not a database or a simple word-frequency counter; the learned vectors encode contextual usage patterns.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":2,"question":"In the context of language models, what does an auto regressive model predict?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The probability of the next token in a text given the previous tokens."},{"key":"b","text":"The probability of the next token using a Monte Carlo sampling of past tokens."},{"key":"c","text":"The next token solely using recurrent network or LSTM cells."},{"key":"d","text":"The probability of the next token by looking at the previous and future input tokens."}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"An autoregressive model factorizes a sequence into conditional next-token predictions: it estimates the next token from the tokens already seen, then appends the chosen token and repeats. A causal model therefore does not use future tokens when generating the next one. The architecture need not be an RNN; GPT-style Transformers are autoregressive too.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":3,"question":"In large language models, what is the purpose of the attention mechanism?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To measure the importance of the words in the output sequence."},{"key":"b","text":"To determine the order in which words are generated."},{"key":"c","text":"To capture the order of the words in the input sequence."},{"key":"d","text":"To assign weights to each word in the input sequence."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Attention computes relevance scores between tokens and uses those scores to form weighted combinations of their representations. This lets a token incorporate information from other relevant positions, even when they are far apart. Attention weights are not positional encodings: they express content-dependent relationships, while positional information represents order.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":4,"question":"In the transformer architecture, what is the purpose of positional encoding?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To remove redundant information from the input sequence."},{"key":"b","text":"To encode the semantic meaning of each token in the input sequence."},{"key":"c","text":"To add information about the order of each token in the input sequence."},{"key":"d","text":"To encode the importance of each token in the input sequence."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A Transformer processes token representations with attention and, by itself, does not inherently know their sequence order. Positional encodings add position information to token representations so the model can distinguish “dog bites man” from “man bites dog.” They do not provide the token meaning or calculate attention importance.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":5,"question":"In the field of machine learning, which of the following serves as the fundamental basis for enabling a model to learn from data?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Algorithms and statistical methods."},{"key":"b","text":"The \"Attention is All You Need\" research paper."},{"key":"c","text":"The transformer architecture."},{"key":"d","text":"Large-scale data sets."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Training examples provide the evidence from which a model estimates patterns and relationships. The learning algorithm and model architecture determine how those patterns are fitted, but without representative data there is little for the model to learn. More data is not automatically better if it is noisy, biased, or unrepresentative.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":6,"question":"In a standard machine learning workflow, which key step logically fits between the training and evaluation phases?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Inference."},{"key":"b","text":"Data collection."},{"key":"c","text":"Model deployment."},{"key":"d","text":"Feature engineering."}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":7,"question":"To enhance the transparency of a large language model and mitigate the blackbox problem, which technique is most effective?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Implementing Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) to connect the model to verifiable external knowledge sources."},{"key":"b","text":"Relying solely on topical guardrails to limit the model's conversation scope."},{"key":"c","text":"Publishing all models and software on a platform like NVIDIA's NGC."},{"key":"d","text":"Increasing the size of the training data set to improve statistical accuracy."}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"RAG can make an answer easier to trace by retrieving passages from an identifiable knowledge source and supplying them to the language model. The retrieved passages can be inspected or cited, which improves evidence traceability. It does not make the model’s internal computations fully explainable, and guardrails or publishing a model do not by themselves reveal why it produced an answer.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":8,"question":"When validating an AI system that processes sensitive user data, what is a crucial step for building trust and ensuring security and reliability?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Achieving compliance with a recognized standard like ISO/IEC 27001 for information security management.,,"},{"key":"b","text":"Using the NVIDIA TAO toolkit with transfer learning."},{"key":"c","text":"Deploying the application using Helm charts for Kubernetes management."},{"key":"d","text":"Maximizing the model's F1 score."}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"ISO/IEC 27001 is an information-security management standard. Its risk-based controls and governance practices help an organization manage access, protect sensitive information, and continually improve security. A toolkit, deployment chart, or high model score is not a substitute for an organization-wide security management system.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":9,"question":"Which library is specifically designed and optimized for linguistic processing tasks like tokenization, lemmatization, and part-of-speech (POS) tagging?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"spaCy."},{"key":"b","text":"pandas."},{"key":"c","text":"scikit-learn."},{"key":"d","text":"Matplotlib."}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"spaCy is a production-oriented NLP library with pipelines for tokenization, part-of-speech tagging, lemmatization, and named-entity recognition. pandas is for tabular data, Matplotlib is for plotting, and scikit-learn focuses on general machine-learning tools rather than a complete high-performance linguistic pipeline.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":10,"question":"Which library is most suitable to accelerate a pandas workflow on an NVIDIA GPU with minimal changes to existing code?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"cuDF."},{"key":"b","text":"NumPy."},{"key":"c","text":"Apache Spark."},{"key":"d","text":"Dask."}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"cuDF is the RAPIDS DataFrame library: it provides a pandas-like API while executing many tabular operations on NVIDIA GPUs. This can accelerate data loading, filtering, joins, and transformations with relatively familiar code. NumPy is a general numerical array library, while Spark and Dask address broader distributed-computing workflows.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":11,"question":"Which applications and corresponding evaluation metrics are appropriate for comparing generated text against human-created reference texts? (Choose two)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Application: image classification; Metric: accuracy."},{"key":"b","text":"Application: machine translation; Metric: BLEU."},{"key":"c","text":"Application: sentiment analysis; Metric: F1 score."},{"key":"d","text":"Application: text summarization; Metric: ROUGE."}],"answers":["b","d"],"multi":true,"explanation":"BLEU and ROUGE compare generated text with one or more human-written reference texts using overlapping n-grams. BLEU is traditionally associated with machine translation and emphasizes precision; ROUGE is widely used for summarization and emphasizes recall/coverage. They are reference-based text-generation metrics, unlike classification metrics such as accuracy or F1.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":12,"question":"Which learning technique describes a model identifying a new class (e.g., an okapi) it has never seen during training, using only a text description?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"One-shot learning."},{"key":"b","text":"Reinforcement learning."},{"key":"c","text":"Zero-shot learning."},{"key":"d","text":"Supervised learning."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Zero-shot prompting asks the model to perform a task without showing any worked examples in the prompt. The instruction and task description alone define what to do. One-shot includes one example, while few-shot includes several examples; fine-tuning changes model parameters rather than merely adding examples to a prompt.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":13,"question":"What are the primary advantages of using a multi-GPU configuration compared to a single GPU? (Choose two)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"It inherently increases the final predictive accuracy of the model."},{"key":"b","text":"It reduces the time required for the training process."},{"key":"c","text":"It eliminates the need for data pre-processing."},{"key":"d","text":"It enables the training of larger models that exceed the memory capacity of a single GPU."}],"answers":["b","d"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Multiple GPUs can distribute computation and model state across devices, shortening training time and making models or batches larger than one GPU’s memory practical. They do not automatically improve predictive accuracy or remove data preparation. Scaling depends on communication overhead, parallelization strategy, and hardware.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":14,"question":"If a real-time AI service has a bottleneck in the data encoding step, what is the most direct outcome of accelerating this step with a GPU?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Predictive accuracy will increase."},{"key":"b","text":"The model will require less training data."},{"key":"c","text":"Operational costs will be completely eliminated."},{"key":"d","text":"The overall latency of the service will decrease."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"If encoding is on the critical path, accelerating it reduces the time spent waiting for that stage and can lower end-to-end latency. The gain is limited by the fraction of total time that encoding consumes (Amdahl’s law). Faster preprocessing does not directly change model accuracy or eliminate operating costs.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":15,"question":"Within the transformer architecture, what specific component is introduced to provide the model with information about word order and position?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The feed-forward network."},{"key":"b","text":"Word embeddings."},{"key":"c","text":"Positional encoding."},{"key":"d","text":"Attention mechanism."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Word2Vec learns a dense vector for each word from the words that tend to appear around it. Words used in similar contexts acquire vectors that are close in the embedding space. It is not a database or a simple word-frequency counter; the learned vectors encode contextual usage patterns.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":16,"question":"Which NVIDIA framework is specifically designed to facilitate building, training, and customizing large language models (LLMs)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NVIDIA Triton Inference Server."},{"key":"b","text":"NVIDIA TensorRT."},{"key":"c","text":"NVIDIA NeMo Framework."},{"key":"d","text":"NVIDIA cuDF."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":17,"question":"Which NVIDIA SDK is specifically designed for deploying high-performance conversational AI services like speech-to-text and text-to-speech?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NVIDIA Omniverse."},{"key":"b","text":"NVIDIA NeMo Framework."},{"key":"c","text":"NVIDIA cuDF."},{"key":"d","text":"NVIDIA Riva."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"cuDF is the RAPIDS DataFrame library: it provides a pandas-like API while executing many tabular operations on NVIDIA GPUs. This can accelerate data loading, filtering, joins, and transformations with relatively familiar code. NumPy is a general numerical array library, while Spark and Dask address broader distributed-computing workflows.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":18,"question":"Which NVIDIA tool is a scalable solution designed to deploy and manage multiple trained AI models regardless of their original framework?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NVIDIA NeMo Framework."},{"key":"b","text":"NVIDIA Triton Inference Server."},{"key":"c","text":"NVIDIA TAO Toolkit."},{"key":"d","text":"NVIDIA TensorRT."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":19,"question":"Which open-source toolkit from NVIDIA is designed to add programmable safety controls (e.g., preventing prompt injections) to a chatbot?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NVIDIA Triton Inference Server."},{"key":"b","text":"NVIDIA TensorRT."},{"key":"c","text":"NeMo Guardrails."},{"key":"d","text":"NVIDIA cuML."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":20,"question":"Which embedding model is designed to generate contextualized word representations, allowing it to differentiate meanings of the same word based on surrounding context?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Word2Vec."},{"key":"b","text":"TF-IDF."},{"key":"c","text":"One-hot encoding."},{"key":"d","text":"BERT."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Word2Vec learns a dense vector for each word from the words that tend to appear around it. Words used in similar contexts acquire vectors that are close in the embedding space. It is not a database or a simple word-frequency counter; the learned vectors encode contextual usage patterns.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":21,"question":"Which of the following is a tree-based ensemble algorithm widely recognized for its high performance and efficiency in prediction tasks using structured tabular data?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"k-means clustering"},{"key":"b","text":"A large-scale transformer model"},{"key":"c","text":"XGBoost"},{"key":"d","text":"Linear regression"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"XGBoost is a gradient-boosted decision-tree algorithm. It builds trees sequentially, with each new tree helping correct errors made by the existing ensemble, and is highly effective on structured/tabular data. k-means is clustering, linear regression is a simple linear predictor, and a Transformer is a neural architecture.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":22,"question":"When evaluating the performance of a language model, what does a lower perplexity score signify?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The model has a larger number of parameters."},{"key":"b","text":"The model is more surprised by the test data."},{"key":"c","text":"The model was trained on a smaller data set."},{"key":"d","text":"The model is more confident and accurate in its predictions."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Perplexity measures how well a language model assigns probability to a text sequence. Lower perplexity means the model assigns higher probability to the observed tokens and is less “surprised” by them. It is not a direct measure of factual correctness, fairness, or model size, and comparisons require comparable tokenization and evaluation data.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":23,"question":"In the context of natural language processing, what is a key distinction between the perplexity metric and the BLEU score?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A lower score is better for BLEU while a higher score is better for perplexity."},{"key":"b","text":"Perplexity is used for image classification while BLEU is used for machine translation."},{"key":"c","text":"Perplexity evaluates how well a language model predicts a sequence of text, while BLEU compares a model's generated output against a human reference."},{"key":"d","text":"Both metrics are identical and can be used interchangeably to evaluate any NLP task."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"BLEU and ROUGE compare generated text with one or more human-written reference texts using overlapping n-grams. BLEU is traditionally associated with machine translation and emphasizes precision; ROUGE is widely used for summarization and emphasizes recall/coverage. They are reference-based text-generation metrics, unlike classification metrics such as accuracy or F1.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":24,"question":"To mitigate hallucinations and ground a large language model's responses in verifiable external facts, a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system is implemented. Which of the following components are essential and work together to achieve this? (Choose two)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The generator, which is an LLM that synthesizes the final answer based on the augmented prompt."},{"key":"b","text":"The fine-tuning module, which continuously retrains the LLM on new data."},{"key":"c","text":"The retriever, which searches a knowledge base for relevant information to augment the prompt."},{"key":"d","text":"The output validator, which checks the generated text for grammatical errors."}],"answers":["a","c"],"multi":true,"explanation":"A basic RAG pipeline has two essential roles: a retriever searches an external knowledge source for relevant passages, and a generator (usually an LLM) uses those passages plus the user’s question to compose an answer. Fine-tuning is a separate model-adaptation process; grammatical validation alone does not supply missing evidence.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":25,"question":"Which NVIDIA platform is specifically designed for an end-to-end GPU-accelerated data science pipeline, offering a familiar Python API similar to pandas and scikit-learn?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Apache Spark"},{"key":"b","text":"Matplotlib"},{"key":"c","text":"NVIDIA RAPIDS"},{"key":"d","text":"NVIDIA NeMo Framework"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"cuDF is the RAPIDS DataFrame library: it provides a pandas-like API while executing many tabular operations on NVIDIA GPUs. This can accelerate data loading, filtering, joins, and transformations with relatively familiar code. NumPy is a general numerical array library, while Spark and Dask address broader distributed-computing workflows.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":26,"question":"In the initial phase of a data science project, what are the primary objectives of Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)? (Choose two)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To deploy the final machine learning model into a production environment."},{"key":"b","text":"To identify potential anomalies, errors, and missing values within the data set."},{"key":"c","text":"To select the final hyperparameters for the model through grid search."},{"key":"d","text":"To summarize the data's main characteristics and uncover initial patterns and relationships."}],"answers":["b","d"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Exploratory Data Analysis examines a dataset before modeling. Summary statistics and visualizations help reveal distributions, relationships, outliers, missing values, inconsistent records, and possible bias. EDA informs cleaning and modeling decisions; it is not deployment or a substitute for later validation and hyperparameter tuning.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":27,"question":"The core mechanism of a diffusion model involves two distinct phases. What are these two phases?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A compression phase (downsizing) and a rendering phase (upscaling)."},{"key":"b","text":"A forward process where noise is progressively added to an image and a reverse process where a model learns to denoise it."},{"key":"c","text":"A feature extraction phase using a convolutional network and a classification phase using a feed-forward network."},{"key":"d","text":"A generator phase that creates an image and a discriminator phase that validates its authenticity."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A basic RAG pipeline has two essential roles: a retriever searches an external knowledge source for relevant passages, and a generator (usually an LLM) uses those passages plus the user’s question to compose an answer. Fine-tuning is a separate model-adaptation process; grammatical validation alone does not supply missing evidence.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":28,"question":"Which NVIDIA SDK is specifically designed to maximize throughput and minimize latency during inference on NVIDIA GPUs without changing the model's architecture?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NVIDIA Triton Inference Server"},{"key":"b","text":"NVIDIA NeMo Framework"},{"key":"c","text":"NVIDIA TensorRT"},{"key":"d","text":"scikit-learn"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":29,"question":"Which open standard should be used to represent a machine learning model to ensure interoperability, allowing a model trained in one framework (like PyTorch) to be used in another (like TensorFlow)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NVIDIA TensorRT"},{"key":"b","text":"ONNX (Open Neural Network Exchange)"},{"key":"c","text":"A Docker container"},{"key":"d","text":"A Python pickle file"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NVIDIA TensorRT is an inference optimization SDK. It can optimize a trained network through graph transformations, kernel selection, precision choices, and other hardware-aware optimizations to improve throughput and latency. Triton is the serving system that can host models; NeMo focuses on model development and customization.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":30,"question":"In a neural network, what are the primary roles of an activation function? (Choose two)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To introduce nonlinear properties, allowing the network to learn complex data patterns."},{"key":"b","text":"To group the input data into distinct clusters similar to the k-means algorithm."},{"key":"c","text":"To determine the output signal of a neuron, deciding whether it should be activated based on its weighted input."},{"key":"d","text":"To normalize the input data to have a mean of zero and a standard deviation of one."},{"key":"e","text":"To directly calculate and apply the weight and bias updates during back propagation."}],"answers":["a","c"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Activation functions transform a neuron’s weighted input. Nonlinear activations such as ReLU let stacked layers represent nonlinear relationships; without nonlinearity, many layers collapse mathematically into one linear transformation. For binary classification, sigmoid is commonly used at the output to map a logit to a value between 0 and 1, while ReLU is common in hidden layers.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":31,"question":"Which of the following algorithms is most suitable for an unsupervised clustering task where the data set does not contain any pre-existing labels?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Linear regression"},{"key":"b","text":"A classification algorithm like Support Vector Machine (SVM)"},{"key":"c","text":"Generative Adversarial Network (GAN)"},{"key":"d","text":"k-means clustering"}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"k-means is an unsupervised clustering algorithm: it groups unlabeled examples around k centroids by repeatedly assigning points to their nearest centroid and updating the centroids. Classification and regression require target labels; a GAN is a generative model, not the standard choice for partitioning unlabeled tabular observations.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":32,"question":"Which natural language processing (NLP) task is specifically designed to extract and categorize specific pieces of information such as company names, executive names, and monetary values?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Sentiment analysis"},{"key":"b","text":"Text summarization"},{"key":"c","text":"Machine translation"},{"key":"d","text":"Named Entity Recognition (NER)"}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"spaCy is a production-oriented NLP library with pipelines for tokenization, part-of-speech tagging, lemmatization, and named-entity recognition. pandas is for tabular data, Matplotlib is for plotting, and scikit-learn focuses on general machine-learning tools rather than a complete high-performance linguistic pipeline.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":33,"question":"When optimizing a deep learning model for deployment, what are the primary advantages of applying model quantization? (Choose two)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A significant increase in the model's predictive accuracy."},{"key":"b","text":"Faster inference speed due to the use of lower-precision integer arithmetic."},{"key":"c","text":"A reduced memory footprint and smaller model file size."},{"key":"d","text":"It simplifies the process of data collection and pre-processing."},{"key":"e","text":"It allows the model to be trained with much less data."}],"answers":["b","c"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":34,"question":"The process of model quantization often involves converting weights from FP32 to int8. What are the direct consequences of this process? (Choose three)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Increased computational throughput and lower inference latency."},{"key":"b","text":"A reduction in the numerical precision of the model's parameters."},{"key":"c","text":"A guaranteed improvement in the model's predictive accuracy."},{"key":"d","text":"Smaller model size and reduced memory (RAM/VRAM) requirements."},{"key":"e","text":"A significant increase in the time required to train the model."}],"answers":["a","b","d"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":35,"question":"To evaluate a binary classification model's performance by providing a balance between precision and recall, which of the following metrics is most suitable?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"BLEU score"},{"key":"b","text":"Perplexity"},{"key":"c","text":"Mean Absolute Error (MAE)"},{"key":"d","text":"F1 score"}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"BLEU and ROUGE compare generated text with one or more human-written reference texts using overlapping n-grams. BLEU is traditionally associated with machine translation and emphasizes precision; ROUGE is widely used for summarization and emphasizes recall/coverage. They are reference-based text-generation metrics, unlike classification metrics such as accuracy or F1.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":36,"question":"Why might accuracy be a poor or misleading metric for evaluating a model's performance on an imbalanced data set (e.g., a fraud detection task where only 1% of cases are fraud)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Accuracy is only used for regression tasks, not classification."},{"key":"b","text":"A model that simply predicts \"not fraud\" for every transaction would also achieve 99% accuracy."},{"key":"c","text":"99% accuracy is generally considered too low for financial applications."},{"key":"d","text":"The F1 score must be calculated first to determine the accuracy."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":37,"question":"Which technology enables the partitioning of a single physical GPU into multiple independent, securely isolated instances with guaranteed portions of compute and memory?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NVLink"},{"key":"b","text":"Data parallelism"},{"key":"c","text":"NVIDIA RAPIDS"},{"key":"d","text":"Multi-Instance GPU (MIG)"}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"cuDF is the RAPIDS DataFrame library: it provides a pandas-like API while executing many tabular operations on NVIDIA GPUs. This can accelerate data loading, filtering, joins, and transformations with relatively familiar code. NumPy is a general numerical array library, while Spark and Dask address broader distributed-computing workflows.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":38,"question":"What is the experimental method called where you randomly show version A (e.g., a blue button) to one group and version B (e.g., a green button) to another and compare the results?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"k-means clustering"},{"key":"b","text":"Decision tree analysis"},{"key":"c","text":"A/B testing"},{"key":"d","text":"Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Exploratory Data Analysis examines a dataset before modeling. Summary statistics and visualizations help reveal distributions, relationships, outliers, missing values, inconsistent records, and possible bias. EDA informs cleaning and modeling decisions; it is not deployment or a substitute for later validation and hyperparameter tuning.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":39,"question":"Which of the following statements accurately describe the characteristics of a single decision tree algorithm? (Choose two)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"It is an unsupervised learning algorithm used primarily for clustering data without labels."},{"key":"b","text":"It can be used for both classification and regression tasks."},{"key":"c","text":"It is a type of deep neural network that uses activation functions and back propagation."},{"key":"d","text":"It makes predictions by learning a series of simple if-then-else rules from the data features."},{"key":"e","text":"It operates by building a multitude of trees on random subsets of data and averaging their predictions."}],"answers":["b","d"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Activation functions transform a neuron’s weighted input. Nonlinear activations such as ReLU let stacked layers represent nonlinear relationships; without nonlinearity, many layers collapse mathematically into one linear transformation. For binary classification, sigmoid is commonly used at the output to map a logit to a value between 0 and 1, while ReLU is common in hidden layers.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":40,"question":"What is the text normalization technique called that transforms different forms of a word (like \"is,\" \"am,\" \"are\") back to their base dictionary form or lemma (like \"be\")?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Stemming"},{"key":"b","text":"Tokenization"},{"key":"c","text":"Named Entity Recognition (NER)"},{"key":"d","text":"Lemmatization"}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"spaCy is a production-oriented NLP library with pipelines for tokenization, part-of-speech tagging, lemmatization, and named-entity recognition. pandas is for tabular data, Matplotlib is for plotting, and scikit-learn focuses on general machine-learning tools rather than a complete high-performance linguistic pipeline.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":41,"question":"Given the sentence \"The quick brown fox jumps,\" what is the correct set of bigrams (2-grams)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"the, quick, brown, fox, jumps"},{"key":"b","text":"A similarity score used to compare it with another sentence"},{"key":"c","text":"{the quick, quick brown, brown fox, fox jumps}"},{"key":"d","text":"the quick brown, quick brown fox, brown fox jumps"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A bigram is a contiguous sequence of two tokens. For “the quick brown fox jumps,” slide a two-word window one position at a time: “the quick,” “quick brown,” “brown fox,” and “fox jumps.” A unigram is one token; a trigram is three.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":42,"question":"Which of the following statements correctly describe the characteristics and typical use cases of Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and t-Distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding (t-SNE)? (Choose two)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"t-SNE is primarily used for preserving the local structure of the data, making it excellent for visualizing distinct clusters."},{"key":"b","text":"PCA is a supervised learning technique that requires labeled data to perform dimensionality reduction."},{"key":"c","text":"Both PCA and t-SNE are guaranteed to find the same patterns and produce nearly identical visualizations."},{"key":"d","text":"PCA is a linear technique that aims to capture the maximum variance in the data, making it effective for understanding the global data structure."},{"key":"e","text":"The primary purpose of both techniques is to train a predictive model for classification."}],"answers":["a","d"],"multi":true,"explanation":"PCA is a linear dimensionality-reduction method that finds orthogonal directions capturing as much variance as possible; it is useful for compact representations and broad/global structure. t-SNE is a nonlinear visualization technique designed mainly to preserve local neighborhood relationships in 2D/3D. t-SNE plots are useful for exploring clusters but can distort global distances and should not be treated as a predictive model.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":43,"question":"In the field of natural language processing (NLP), what is the correct term for a large, structured collection of text documents used for training language models and conducting linguistic research?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Hyperparameter"},{"key":"b","text":"Checkpoint"},{"key":"c","text":"Corpus"},{"key":"d","text":"Algorithm"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A corpus is a structured collection of text or speech samples used for linguistic analysis and for training or evaluating language technologies. A checkpoint is a saved model state, a hyperparameter controls training, and an algorithm is a procedure rather than the dataset itself.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":44,"question":"What is the primary purpose of the technique known as dropout during the training of neural networks?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To introduce nonlinearity into the network, allowing it to learn complex patterns."},{"key":"b","text":"To permanently remove unimportant neurons after training to reduce the model's file size."},{"key":"c","text":"To prevent the model from overfitting by making it learn more robust and redundant features."},{"key":"d","text":"To calculate the gradient of the loss function with respect to the model's weights."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Dropout is a regularization technique used during training: it randomly disables a subset of neuron activations on each training pass. This discourages co-adaptation and encourages more robust representations, helping reduce overfitting. It does not permanently delete neurons or calculate gradients.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":45,"question":"While recurrent neural networks (RNNs) are designed for sequential data, in which of the following applications are convolutional neural networks (CNNs) most widely and effectively used?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Time series forecasting"},{"key":"b","text":"Image classification and object detection"},{"key":"c","text":"Customer segmentation using k-means"},{"key":"d","text":"Predicting housing prices from a tabular data set"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"k-means is an unsupervised clustering algorithm: it groups unlabeled examples around k centroids by repeatedly assigning points to their nearest centroid and updating the centroids. Classification and regression require target labels; a GAN is a generative model, not the standard choice for partitioning unlabeled tabular observations.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":46,"question":"What is the key architectural feature of a recurrent neural network (RNN) that enables it to process sequential data?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A set of convolutional layers designed to detect spatial patterns."},{"key":"b","text":"A purely feed-forward structure where information only moves in one direction without loops."},{"key":"c","text":"The exclusive use of linear activation functions throughout the network."},{"key":"d","text":"A feedback loop or hidden state that passes information from one time step to the next."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Activation functions transform a neuron’s weighted input. Nonlinear activations such as ReLU let stacked layers represent nonlinear relationships; without nonlinearity, many layers collapse mathematically into one linear transformation. For binary classification, sigmoid is commonly used at the output to map a logit to a value between 0 and 1, while ReLU is common in hidden layers.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":47,"question":"During the training of a neural network, what is the primary purpose of the backpropagation algorithm?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To perform the forward pass where input data is fed through the network to generate a prediction."},{"key":"b","text":"To randomly deactivate neurons in a layer to prevent the model from overfitting."},{"key":"c","text":"To initialize the weights and biases of the network before the training process begins."},{"key":"d","text":"To calculate the gradient of the loss function with respect to the network's weights, enabling learning."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":48,"question":"What is the primary purpose of the NVIDIA NGC catalog?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"An online marketplace for buying and selling third-party software licenses."},{"key":"b","text":"A source code repository for collaborating on open-source projects similar to GitHub."},{"key":"c","text":"A cloud computing platform for renting virtual machines equipped with GPUs."},{"key":"d","text":"A central hub for accessing GPU-accelerated software such as containers, pre-trained models, and SDKs."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":49,"question":"In the context of training a machine learning model, what is the correct definition of one epoch?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A single forward and backward pass of one batch of data through the network."},{"key":"b","text":"The final evaluation of the model's performance on the test data set."},{"key":"c","text":"One complete pass through the entire training data set."},{"key":"d","text":"The process of tuning the model's hyperparameters such as the learning rate."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"One epoch means the model has processed every example in the training dataset once. If the dataset is split into batches, an epoch contains multiple training steps/iterations. A single batch pass is not a full epoch, and evaluation or hyperparameter tuning are separate activities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":50,"question":"What is the primary role of the NVIDIA Base Command Platform in an AI-powered data center?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Hardware interconnect for linking multiple DGX systems with high bandwidth."},{"key":"b","text":"A command-line interface for monitoring the status of a single NVIDIA GPU."},{"key":"c","text":"The software suite for managing AI development workflows and orchestrating DGX infrastructure."},{"key":"d","text":"A library for accelerating data processing tasks similar to pandas but on the GPU."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NVIDIA Base Command Platform provides software for managing AI development workflows and orchestrating DGX infrastructure, including team resources and job workflows. It operates above the hardware layer; it is not an interconnect like NVLink or a dataframe library like cuDF.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":51,"question":"Which software solution is specifically designed to meet enterprise-grade requirements such as long-term stability, robust security, and professional support (SLAs)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Downloading the latest open-source packages directly from public repositories."},{"key":"b","text":"NVIDIA AI Enterprise"},{"key":"c","text":"A standard cloud virtual machine with a base operating system."},{"key":"d","text":"A single open-source framework like PyTorch."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NVIDIA AI Enterprise is an enterprise software platform for production AI, emphasizing supported and tested software, security, stability, and enterprise support. A collection of unpinned public packages or one standalone open-source framework does not by itself provide the same integrated support and service-level commitments.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":52,"question":"Which NVIDIA offering is designed to package generative AI models as easy-to-use, scalable microservices with standard APIs to simplify integration?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NVIDIA DGX system"},{"key":"b","text":"NVIDIA NIM (NVIDIA Inference Microservices)"},{"key":"c","text":"NVIDIA NeMo Framework"},{"key":"d","text":"NVIDIA Triton Inference Server"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":54,"question":"Which NVIDIA offering provides a collection of optimized microservices specifically for core RAG tasks, including embedding documents and searching for relevant context?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NVIDIA Omniverse"},{"key":"b","text":"NeMo Guardrails"},{"key":"c","text":"NVIDIA NeMo Retriever"},{"key":"d","text":"A custom Python script using scikit-learn"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NeMo Guardrails adds programmable conversational controls around an LLM application. Rails can constrain allowed topics and flows, check inputs or outputs, and help defend against risks such as prompt injection. It is an application safety layer, not an inference optimizer or a data-science library.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":55,"question":"Which combination of tools is most appropriate for building a complex generative AI application (the entire end-to-end workflow) and connecting different AI models/tools (agent logic)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Use NVIDIA AI workflows for connecting models and LangChain for building the application."},{"key":"b","text":"Use NVIDIA AI workflows for both building the application and connecting the models."},{"key":"c","text":"Use NVIDIA AI workflows for building the application and LangChain for connecting the models."},{"key":"d","text":"Use LangChain for both building the application and connecting the models."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":56,"question":"Which of the following components and techniques are key to the parallel processing capability of the transformer architecture? (Choose two)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A recurrent feedback loop that processes tokens one by one in a strict sequence."},{"key":"b","text":"Multi-head attention, which allows different attention heads to be computed independently and in parallel."},{"key":"c","text":"The model's ability to process all input tokens simultaneously rather than sequentially."},{"key":"d","text":"A k-means clustering algorithm to group similar tokens before processing."},{"key":"e","text":"A single attention mechanism that must process all relationships serially."}],"answers":["b","c"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Attention computes relevance scores between tokens and uses those scores to form weighted combinations of their representations. This lets a token incorporate information from other relevant positions, even when they are far apart. Attention weights are not positional encodings: they express content-dependent relationships, while positional information represents order.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":57,"question":"Which technology is specifically designed to protect sensitive data while it is actively being processed in memory during use?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Standard software-based encryption"},{"key":"b","text":"A virtual private network (VPN)"},{"key":"c","text":"Confidential Computing"},{"key":"d","text":"A network firewall"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Confidential Computing protects data while it is being used, typically by processing it inside hardware-backed trusted execution environments that isolate memory and computation. Encryption at rest protects stored data; TLS/VPNs protect data in transit; a firewall controls network traffic. Those controls do not alone protect data during processing.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":58,"question":"Which activation function is most suitable for the output layer of a binary classification problem to represent a probability (0 to 1)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit)"},{"key":"b","text":"Tanh (Hyperbolic Tangent)"},{"key":"c","text":"Softmax"},{"key":"d","text":"Sigmoid"}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Activation functions transform a neuron’s weighted input. Nonlinear activations such as ReLU let stacked layers represent nonlinear relationships; without nonlinearity, many layers collapse mathematically into one linear transformation. For binary classification, sigmoid is commonly used at the output to map a logit to a value between 0 and 1, while ReLU is common in hidden layers.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":59,"question":"Which of the following is a popular parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) method that freezes the original model weights and only trains a small number of new adapter parameters?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Full fine-tuning"},{"key":"b","text":"k-means clustering"},{"key":"c","text":"LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation)"},{"key":"d","text":"Post-training quantization (PTQ)"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NVIDIA NeMo is a framework for building, customizing, training, and adapting generative AI models, including LLMs. It supports workflows such as prompt engineering, parameter-efficient fine-tuning, and full fine-tuning. Triton is primarily for serving models, while TensorRT optimizes inference execution.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":60,"question":"What is the primary characteristic that distinguishes generative AI from other types of AI such as predictive or analytical AI?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Its ability to cluster unlabeled data into distinct groups."},{"key":"b","text":"Its ability to classify existing data into predefined categories."},{"key":"c","text":"Its ability to create new, original content such as text, images, or music that resembles the data it was trained on."},{"key":"d","text":"Its ability to predict a continuous numerical value based on input features."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":62,"question":"While the Word2Vec model is used to learn vector representations for individual words, the Doc2Vec model is an extension of this technique. What is the primary purpose of Doc2Vec?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To generate a numerical vector representation for each individual word in the vocabulary."},{"key":"b","text":"To classify a document into predefined categories like sports or politics."},{"key":"c","text":"To generate a single numerical vector that represents the semantic meaning of an entire document or paragraph."},{"key":"d","text":"To create a new synthetically generated document that is stylistically similar to the input."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Word2Vec learns a dense vector for each word from the words that tend to appear around it. Words used in similar contexts acquire vectors that are close in the embedding space. It is not a database or a simple word-frequency counter; the learned vectors encode contextual usage patterns.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":63,"question":"Which of the following options correctly lists the steps of a typical machine learning workflow in the proper sequential order?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Model training, model evaluation, data collection, data prep-processing, model deployment."},{"key":"b","text":"Data collection, model deployment, data prep-processing, model training, model evaluation."},{"key":"c","text":"Data collection, data prep-processing, model training, model evaluation, model deployment."},{"key":"d","text":"Data prep-processing, data collection, model training, model deployment, model evaluation."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":65,"question":"What is the primary purpose of a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To classify input data into one of several predefined categories."},{"key":"b","text":"To generate new synthetic data samples that mimic a real data set."},{"key":"c","text":"To predict a continuous numerical value based on a set of input features."},{"key":"d","text":"To group unlabeled data points into clusters based on their similarity."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":66,"question":"How does an auto regressive language model, such as a GPT-style model, generate text?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"It analyzes the entire input text at once, considering both past and future words to understand context."},{"key":"b","text":"It classifies the entire text into a single category such as positive or negative."},{"key":"c","text":"It generates the next token in a sequence based only on the sequence of tokens that it has generated in previous steps."},{"key":"d","text":"It groups similar documents together into clusters without using any labels."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"An autoregressive model factorizes a sequence into conditional next-token predictions: it estimates the next token from the tokens already seen, then appends the chosen token and repeats. A causal model therefore does not use future tokens when generating the next one. The architecture need not be an RNN; GPT-style Transformers are autoregressive too.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":67,"question":"What is the primary application of the t-SNE (t-distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding) algorithm in data science?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To train a model to classify data into predefined categories."},{"key":"b","text":"To normalize the scale of features in a data set before model training."},{"key":"c","text":"To generate new synthetic data samples that are similar to a real data set."},{"key":"d","text":"To visualize high-dimensional data in a low-dimensional space such as 2D or 3D to reveal underlying structures and clusters."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":68,"question":"In the context of evaluating the trustworthiness of an AI model, what is the key difference between reliability and validity?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Reliability measures if the model produces consistent results, while validity measures if the model is actually correct and measures what it intends to."},{"key":"b","text":"Reliability measures the model's accuracy on the training data, while validity measures its accuracy on the test data."},{"key":"c","text":"Reliability refers to the model's inference speed, while validity refers to its memory usage."},{"key":"d","text":"Both terms mean the same thing and can be used interchangeably to describe a model's overall accuracy."}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":69,"question":"In a deep learning workflow, data is often loaded on the CPU and then transferred to the GPU for training. When this transfer becomes a bottleneck, which memory optimization technique can significantly improve throughput?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Using a larger batch size."},{"key":"b","text":"Applying data augmentation."},{"key":"c","text":"Using pinned memory (page-locked memory)."},{"key":"d","text":"Increasing the number of layers in the neural network."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"If encoding is on the critical path, accelerating it reduces the time spent waiting for that stage and can lower end-to-end latency. The gain is limited by the fraction of total time that encoding consumes (Amdahl’s law). Faster preprocessing does not directly change model accuracy or eliminate operating costs.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":70,"question":"What is a key shared characteristic between the XGBoost library and the NVIDIA RAPIDS cuML library?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Both are designed exclusively for deep learning and neural network tasks."},{"key":"b","text":"Both are primarily used for unsupervised learning tasks like clustering."},{"key":"c","text":"Both can be significantly accelerated using NVIDIA GPUs."},{"key":"d","text":"Both are core components of the Apache Spark ecosystem for distributed computing."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"cuDF is the RAPIDS DataFrame library: it provides a pandas-like API while executing many tabular operations on NVIDIA GPUs. This can accelerate data loading, filtering, joins, and transformations with relatively familiar code. NumPy is a general numerical array library, while Spark and Dask address broader distributed-computing workflows.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":71,"question":"What is the correct term for the phenomenon where an AI model generates a factually incorrect or nonsensical statement but presents it confidently as if it were true?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Overfitting."},{"key":"b","text":"Bias."},{"key":"c","text":"Hallucination."},{"key":"d","text":"Quantization error."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":72,"question":"Select two models or techniques that are used to create numerical vector representations (embeddings) of text. (Choose two)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"k-means clustering."},{"key":"b","text":"BERT."},{"key":"c","text":"Back propagation."},{"key":"d","text":"Doc2Vec."},{"key":"e","text":"A standard convolutional neural network (CNN)."}],"answers":["b","d"],"multi":true,"explanation":"BERT creates contextual token representations: the vector for a word depends on the surrounding words in that particular sentence. Thus “bank” can receive different representations in “river bank” and “bank account.” Traditional Word2Vec usually assigns one static vector per word, regardless of sentence context.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":73,"question":"To adhere to the principle of privacy in trustworthy AI, what is a fundamental practice a company must implement when using personal user data for training?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Collect as much user data as possible to maximize model accuracy."},{"key":"b","text":"Use the data for training without explicitly informing users to speed up development."},{"key":"c","text":"Provide users with clear information and the ability to consent to or opt out of their data being used."},{"key":"d","text":"Switch to a more complex model architecture to automatically handle privacy."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"ISO/IEC 27001 is an information-security management standard. Its risk-based controls and governance practices help an organization manage access, protect sensitive information, and continually improve security. A toolkit, deployment chart, or high model score is not a substitute for an organization-wide security management system.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":74,"question":"Which NVIDIA framework provides a comprehensive end-to-end platform that supports techniques ranging from simple prompt engineering to PEFT and full fine-tuning?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NVIDIA Triton Inference Server."},{"key":"b","text":"NVIDIA DGX system."},{"key":"c","text":"NVIDIA TensorRT."},{"key":"d","text":"NVIDIA NeMo framework."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":75,"question":"In the context of AI governance and trustworthy systems, what does certification refer to?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The process of training the AI model on a large, diverse data set."},{"key":"b","text":"An informal code review conducted by the internal development team."},{"key":"c","text":"The formal process of verifying that an AI system meets established standards for aspects like security, fairness, and reliability."},{"key":"d","text":"The process of optimizing the model's code for faster inference speed."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":76,"question":"What is the key feature of the NVIDIA Triton Inference Server that automatically groups incoming real-time requests into larger batches for more efficient GPU processing?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Model fine-tuning."},{"key":"b","text":"Dynamic batching."},{"key":"c","text":"Load balancing."},{"key":"d","text":"Post-training quantization."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":77,"question":"According to the concept of scaling laws in deep learning, what is one of the most reliable ways to predictably improve the performance of a large language model?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Changing the activation function from ReLU to sigmoid."},{"key":"b","text":"Increasing the size of the training data set and the number of model parameters."},{"key":"c","text":"Applying post-training quantization to the model."},{"key":"d","text":"Switching to a different optimization algorithm like from Adam to SGD."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Activation functions transform a neuron’s weighted input. Nonlinear activations such as ReLU let stacked layers represent nonlinear relationships; without nonlinearity, many layers collapse mathematically into one linear transformation. For binary classification, sigmoid is commonly used at the output to map a logit to a value between 0 and 1, while ReLU is common in hidden layers.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":78,"question":"Which of the following statements best describes the primary roles of ONNX and NVIDIA TensorRT in a machine learning workflow?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"ONNX is used to train neural networks while TensorRT is used to collect and pre-process data."},{"key":"b","text":"ONNX is an open format for model interoperability while TensorRT is an SDK for optimizing and deploying models for high-performance inference."},{"key":"c","text":"Both ONNX and TensorRT are used for the same purpose of training models from scratch."},{"key":"d","text":"ONNX is an SDK for optimizing model performance while TensorRT is an open format for model interoperability."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":79,"question":"What are the two primary benefits of applying model quantization to a trained deep learning model? (Choose two)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Increased inference throughput."},{"key":"b","text":"Significantly improved predictive accuracy."},{"key":"c","text":"Reduced model memory requirements."},{"key":"d","text":"Simplified data collection process."},{"key":"e","text":"Reduced model training time."}],"answers":["a","c"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":80,"question":"Which of the following metrics is the industry standard for comparing machine-generated translations against several high-quality human-expert translations?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Accuracy."},{"key":"b","text":"Mean Absolute Error (MAE)."},{"key":"c","text":"Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU) score."},{"key":"d","text":"Perplexity."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"BLEU and ROUGE compare generated text with one or more human-written reference texts using overlapping n-grams. BLEU is traditionally associated with machine translation and emphasizes precision; ROUGE is widely used for summarization and emphasizes recall/coverage. They are reference-based text-generation metrics, unlike classification metrics such as accuracy or F1.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":81,"question":"A company decides to switch from using a 7 billion parameter language model to a much larger 70 billion parameter model for their application. According to AI scaling laws, what are the typical consequences of this change? (Choose two)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The model will require significantly less data to train."},{"key":"b","text":"The model's performance and accuracy will likely increase."},{"key":"c","text":"The model will become less expensive to host and run."},{"key":"d","text":"The model's inference time latency will likely increase."},{"key":"e","text":"The model will be easier to deploy on edge devices with limited memory."}],"answers":["b","d"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":82,"question":"A user gives a large language model the following instruction: \"Translate the following English sentence to French: hello how are you\". The user does not provide any examples of English to French translations in the prompt. What is this type of prompting technique called?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"One-shot prompting"},{"key":"b","text":"Fine-tuning"},{"key":"c","text":"Zero-shot prompting"},{"key":"d","text":"Few-shot prompting"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Zero-shot prompting asks the model to perform a task without showing any worked examples in the prompt. The instruction and task description alone define what to do. One-shot includes one example, while few-shot includes several examples; fine-tuning changes model parameters rather than merely adding examples to a prompt.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":83,"question":"In natural language processing, what is the primary goal of using text normalization techniques like stemming and lemmatization?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To generate new sentences that are grammatically correct."},{"key":"b","text":"To classify the sentiment of a text as positive, negative, or neutral."},{"key":"c","text":"To reduce inflectional forms of a word to a common base or root form."},{"key":"d","text":"To identify and categorize named entities such as persons, organizations, and locations."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":84,"question":"A developer is building a production-grade application that requires fast and efficient natural language processing for tasks like tokenization, part-of-speech tagging, and named entity recognition. Which of the following Python libraries is an industry standard high-performance tool specifically designed for these purposes?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"pandas"},{"key":"b","text":"Matplotlib"},{"key":"c","text":"spaCy"},{"key":"d","text":"scikit-learn"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"spaCy is a production-oriented NLP library with pipelines for tokenization, part-of-speech tagging, lemmatization, and named-entity recognition. pandas is for tabular data, Matplotlib is for plotting, and scikit-learn focuses on general machine-learning tools rather than a complete high-performance linguistic pipeline.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":85,"question":"What is the typical relationship between the ONNX format and NVIDIA TensorRT in a model deployment workflow?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"TensorRT is used to convert a trained model into the ONNX format."},{"key":"b","text":"A model is often exported to the ONNX format, which is then used as an input for optimization by TensorRT."},{"key":"c","text":"ONNX and TensorRT are competing standards that are mutually exclusive and cannot be used together."},{"key":"d","text":"Both ONNX and TensorRT are frameworks used for training neural networks from scratch."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NVIDIA Triton Inference Server is a model-serving system, not a model-training framework. It can serve models from multiple backends/formats (for example, TensorFlow, PyTorch, ONNX Runtime, and TensorRT) and provides production features such as model management, concurrency, and batching. Model files and configuration are supplied through a model repository.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":86,"question":"Before fine-tuning a pre-trained language model on a new custom data set, why is it a crucial first step to perform exploratory data analysis (EDA)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"EDA is the process of deploying the model to a production server."},{"key":"b","text":"EDA helps to understand the data's quality, identify potential issues like biases or anomalies, and inform the fine-tuning strategy."},{"key":"c","text":"EDA is a technique used to significantly increase the number of parameters in the model."},{"key":"d","text":"EDA is an unnecessary step as pre-trained models are already robust enough to handle any type of data."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Exploratory Data Analysis examines a dataset before modeling. Summary statistics and visualizations help reveal distributions, relationships, outliers, missing values, inconsistent records, and possible bias. EDA informs cleaning and modeling decisions; it is not deployment or a substitute for later validation and hyperparameter tuning.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":87,"question":"An insurance company uses an AI model to determine premium rates for customers. It is discovered that the model consistently offers higher rates to applicants from certain neighborhoods, even when their individual risk profiles are identical to applicants from other areas. This is an example of the model violating which key principle of trustworthy AI?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Performance"},{"key":"b","text":"Bias/Fairness"},{"key":"c","text":"Reliability"},{"key":"d","text":"Privacy"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"This scenario illustrates bias and a fairness problem: applicants with equivalent individual risk profiles receive systematically different outcomes because of neighborhood-associated patterns. Fairness evaluation checks whether protected or proxy groups experience unjustified disparities. This is distinct from privacy (data handling), robustness (stability under changing conditions), and raw predictive performance.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":88,"question":"A bank uses a complex deep learning model to approve or deny loan applications. When a customer's application is denied, they ask for the reason; however, the bank is unable to provide a clear explanation because the model's decision-making process is too complex to interpret. This model lacks which important characteristic of trustworthy AI?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Explainability/Transparency"},{"key":"b","text":"Scalability"},{"key":"c","text":"Accuracy"},{"key":"d","text":"Robustness"}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Explainability/transparency concerns whether people can understand the factors or reasoning behind an AI output. If a bank cannot give a meaningful account of why an application was denied, the system lacks adequate explainability. Scalability concerns handling more workload; robustness concerns reliability under changing inputs or conditions.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":89,"question":"A company deploys an AI-powered image recognition system at the entrance of their building. The system works perfectly under normal lighting conditions but consistently fails to recognize employees when it is a very sunny or a very cloudy day. This model is failing in which key area of trustworthy AI?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Privacy"},{"key":"b","text":"Fairness"},{"key":"c","text":"Robustness"},{"key":"d","text":"Transparency"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"This scenario illustrates bias and a fairness problem: applicants with equivalent individual risk profiles receive systematically different outcomes because of neighborhood-associated patterns. Fairness evaluation checks whether protected or proxy groups experience unjustified disparities. This is distinct from privacy (data handling), robustness (stability under changing conditions), and raw predictive performance.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":90,"question":"In the context of a trustworthy AI model, which statement correctly defines validity and reliability?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Validity refers to how fast the model makes a prediction; reliability refers to how much memory it uses."},{"key":"b","text":"Validity means the model produces consistent outputs for the same input; reliability means the model's outputs are factually correct."},{"key":"c","text":"Validity and reliability are the same and both measure the model's overall accuracy."},{"key":"d","text":"Validity means the model's outputs are factually correct and measure what is intended; reliability means the model produces consistent outputs for the same input."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Reliability is consistency: repeated runs under the same conditions should produce stable results. Validity is correctness and fitness for purpose: the system should measure or predict what it is intended to measure. A model can be consistent but consistently wrong, so reliability alone does not establish validity.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":91,"question":"The concept of power law generalization or AI scaling laws describes the relationship between the scale of a model (parameters, data set size, and compute) and its performance. Which statement best describes this relationship?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Model performance increases linearly with the amount of training data without any upper limit."},{"key":"b","text":"Model performance improves predictably as scale increases, but the rate of improvement diminishes."},{"key":"c","text":"A model reaches its optimal performance with a relatively small amount of data and adding more data provides no benefit."},{"key":"d","text":"The performance of a model is completely independent of its size or the amount of training data."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":92,"question":"In distributed data parallel training, each GPU calculates the gradients for its own mini-batch of data. Before the model weights can be updated, these individual gradients must be aggregated and synchronized across all GPUs. What is this collective communication operation called?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Broadcast"},{"key":"b","text":"Scatter"},{"key":"c","text":"All-reduce"},{"key":"d","text":"Data augmentation"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"All-reduce is a collective communication operation in which every participating GPU contributes a tensor (here, gradients) and receives the reduced result. In data-parallel training, this synchronizes gradient information so replicas can apply consistent weight updates. Broadcast sends one source value to all; scatter distributes different chunks.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":93,"question":"What is the primary purpose of applying model quantization in a deep learning workflow?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To increase the predictive accuracy of the model."},{"key":"b","text":"To accelerate inference speed and reduce the model's memory footprint."},{"key":"c","text":"To augment the training data set with new synthetic examples."},{"key":"d","text":"To train a model from scratch with fewer parameters."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":94,"question":"A user provides the following prompt to a large language model: \"Translate English to French: Sea otter = Loutre de mer, Cheese = \". This is an example of which prompting technique?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Fine-tuning"},{"key":"b","text":"Few-shot prompting"},{"key":"c","text":"Zero-shot prompting"},{"key":"d","text":"One-shot prompting"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Zero-shot prompting asks the model to perform a task without showing any worked examples in the prompt. The instruction and task description alone define what to do. One-shot includes one example, while few-shot includes several examples; fine-tuning changes model parameters rather than merely adding examples to a prompt.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":96,"question":"What is the primary purpose of the GLUE (General Language Understanding Evaluation) benchmark in the field of natural language processing?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To provide a large unlabeled corpus for pre-training language models."},{"key":"b","text":"To serve as a standardized suite of diverse NLP tasks for evaluating a model's general language understanding capabilities."},{"key":"c","text":"To be a specific type of model architecture similar to a transformer."},{"key":"d","text":"To be a software library for deploying language models into production."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":97,"question":"While both BLEU and ROUGE are metrics used to evaluate generated text by comparing it to a reference, they are optimized for different tasks. Which statement correctly describes their primary use cases?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"BLEU is for text summarization (recall-focused) while ROUGE is for machine translation (precision-focused)."},{"key":"b","text":"Both metrics are primarily used for evaluating the accuracy of classification models."},{"key":"c","text":"BLEU is for machine translation (precision-focused) while ROUGE is for text summarization (recall-focused)."},{"key":"d","text":"Both metrics are identical and can be used interchangeably for any text generation task."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"BLEU and ROUGE compare generated text with one or more human-written reference texts using overlapping n-grams. BLEU is traditionally associated with machine translation and emphasizes precision; ROUGE is widely used for summarization and emphasizes recall/coverage. They are reference-based text-generation metrics, unlike classification metrics such as accuracy or F1.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":98,"question":"A user asks a large language model: \"Who improved the light bulb after Thomas Edison?\" The model confidently replies: \"After Thomas Edison, the light bulb was significantly improved by Arthur Clark in 1972, who developed the carbon nanofilament.\" Even though Arthur Clark is a science fiction writer and this invention is fictional, what is this phenomenon called?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Overfitting"},{"key":"b","text":"Bias"},{"key":"c","text":"Hallucination"},{"key":"d","text":"Data poisoning"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"This is an AI hallucination: the model generates a plausible-sounding statement that is factually false and presents it with confidence. Language models generate likely continuations from learned patterns; without reliable grounding or verification, they can invent names, dates, and events. Overfitting means fitting training data too closely, bias is a systematic skew in outputs, and data poisoning is the deliberate corruption of training data. A confident tone is not evidence that an answer is true.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":99,"question":"An e-commerce company wants to determine if changing the color of their \"add to cart\" button from blue to green will increase user clicks. They decide to randomly show the original blue button to one group (Group A) and the new green button to another (Group B) and compare results. What is this experimental method called?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"k-means clustering"},{"key":"b","text":"Decision tree analysis"},{"key":"c","text":"A/B testing"},{"key":"d","text":"Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Exploratory Data Analysis examines a dataset before modeling. Summary statistics and visualizations help reveal distributions, relationships, outliers, missing values, inconsistent records, and possible bias. EDA informs cleaning and modeling decisions; it is not deployment or a substitute for later validation and hyperparameter tuning.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":100,"question":"In text pre-processing, both stemming and lemmatization are used to reduce words to their root form. What is the key difference between these two techniques?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Stemming is a more modern technique that always produces more accurate results than lemmatization."},{"key":"b","text":"Stemming uses dictionary and morphological analysis to find a word's base form while lemmatization uses simple rules."},{"key":"c","text":"There is no difference; both are identical techniques."},{"key":"d","text":"Lemmatization uses dictionary and morphological analysis to find a word's base form while stemming uses simple rules to chop off word endings."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Lemmatization uses vocabulary and morphological context to return a valid dictionary lemma (for example, “am,” “is,” and “are” can map to “be”). Stemming usually applies simpler suffix-stripping rules and may produce a fragment that is not a real word. Both normalize word forms, but lemmatization is linguistically richer.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":102,"question":"A data science team wants to accelerate their entire workflow (data loading, manipulation, and machine learning) on GPUs using a familiar Python API similar to pandas and scikit-learn. Which NVIDIA platform is specifically designed for this?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Apache Spark"},{"key":"b","text":"Matplotlib"},{"key":"c","text":"NVIDIA RAPIDS"},{"key":"d","text":"NVIDIA NeMo framework"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"cuDF is the RAPIDS DataFrame library: it provides a pandas-like API while executing many tabular operations on NVIDIA GPUs. This can accelerate data loading, filtering, joins, and transformations with relatively familiar code. NumPy is a general numerical array library, while Spark and Dask address broader distributed-computing workflows.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":103,"question":"When building a neural network for a binary classification task, what are the typical use cases for the sigmoid and ReLU activation functions?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Both are interchangeable and can be used in either the hidden layers or the output layer."},{"key":"b","text":"ReLU is used in the output layer to produce a probability while sigmoid is used in the hidden layers."},{"key":"c","text":"Sigmoid is used in the output layer to produce a probability while ReLU is used in the hidden layers."},{"key":"d","text":"Both are primarily used for regression tasks not classification."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Activation functions transform a neuron’s weighted input. Nonlinear activations such as ReLU let stacked layers represent nonlinear relationships; without nonlinearity, many layers collapse mathematically into one linear transformation. For binary classification, sigmoid is commonly used at the output to map a logit to a value between 0 and 1, while ReLU is common in hidden layers.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":106,"question":"How does the Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) technique primarily enhance the trustworthiness of a large language model's outputs?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"By encrypting the model's training data to ensure privacy."},{"key":"b","text":"By grounding the model's responses in a specific verifiable and up-to-date knowledge base, reducing hallucinations."},{"key":"c","text":"By significantly increasing the number of parameters in the model to make it more intelligent."},{"key":"d","text":"By automatically detecting and removing biases from the model's original training corpus."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":111,"question":"Which of the following metrics is the industry standard for evaluating machine translation quality by comparing it against human expert translations?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Accuracy"},{"key":"b","text":"Mean Absolute Error (MAE)"},{"key":"c","text":"BLEU (Bilingual Evaluation Understudy) score"},{"key":"d","text":"Perplexity"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"BLEU and ROUGE compare generated text with one or more human-written reference texts using overlapping n-grams. BLEU is traditionally associated with machine translation and emphasizes precision; ROUGE is widely used for summarization and emphasizes recall/coverage. They are reference-based text-generation metrics, unlike classification metrics such as accuracy or F1.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":112,"question":"Which technique is specifically designed to learn a dense numerical vector representation for each individual word in a vocabulary based on its surrounding context?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Doc2Vec"},{"key":"b","text":"Word2Vec"},{"key":"c","text":"Sentiment analysis"},{"key":"d","text":"Tokenization"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Word2Vec learns a dense vector for each word from the words that tend to appear around it. Words used in similar contexts acquire vectors that are close in the embedding space. It is not a database or a simple word-frequency counter; the learned vectors encode contextual usage patterns.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":113,"question":"Which option correctly matches the NVIDIA data center GPUs with their respective microarchitectures?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A100-Hopper, H100-Volta, V100-Ampere"},{"key":"b","text":"A100-Volta, H100-Ampere, V100-Hopper"},{"key":"c","text":"A100-Ampere, H100-Hopper, V100-Volta"},{"key":"d","text":"A100-Ampere, H100-Volta, V100-Hopper"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"The NVIDIA A100 is based on the Ampere architecture, the H100 on Hopper, and the V100 on Volta. Remember the chronological mapping: Volta (V100) preceded Ampere (A100), which preceded Hopper (H100). The distractors swap these architecture names.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":114,"question":"What is the NLP task called that automatically determines whether the opinion in a review is positive, negative, or neutral?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Named Entity Recognition (NER)"},{"key":"b","text":"Machine translation"},{"key":"c","text":"Sentiment analysis"},{"key":"d","text":"Text summarization"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"spaCy is a production-oriented NLP library with pipelines for tokenization, part-of-speech tagging, lemmatization, and named-entity recognition. pandas is for tabular data, Matplotlib is for plotting, and scikit-learn focuses on general machine-learning tools rather than a complete high-performance linguistic pipeline.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":115,"question":"Which emerging technology provides a verifiable \"nutrition label\" for AI-generated or human-edited content, securely attaching information about its origin and edit history?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A/B testing"},{"key":"b","text":"SHA-256 hashing"},{"key":"c","text":"Content Credentials (C2PA standard)"},{"key":"d","text":"Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Named Entity Recognition locates spans of text and labels them as entity types such as people, organizations, locations, dates, and monetary amounts. Sentiment analysis predicts opinion polarity, summarization shortens text, and translation changes language; none of those is the task of extracting typed entities.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":116,"question":"Which statement best describes the flexibility of the NVIDIA Triton Inference Server in handling different models?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Triton can only deploy models that were trained using the NVIDIA NeMo framework."},{"key":"b","text":"Triton can deploy models from virtually any framework, such as TensorFlow, PyTorch, TensorRT, and ONNX."},{"key":"c","text":"Triton automatically retrains models if their performance degrades over time."},{"key":"d","text":"Triton is a software library for data processing, not for deploying models."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":117,"question":"Which performance optimization feature of the NVIDIA Triton Inference Server automatically groups individual requests into a single larger batch?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Model versioning"},{"key":"b","text":"Dynamic batching"},{"key":"c","text":"Health monitoring"},{"key":"d","text":"Rate limiting"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":118,"question":"Where does the NVIDIA Triton Inference Server load its models and configurations from?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Directly from a public GitHub repository."},{"key":"b","text":"A local or cloud-based storage location known as the model repository."},{"key":"c","text":"An integrated development environment (IDE) like VS Code."},{"key":"d","text":"The NVIDIA NGC catalog exclusively."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Inference is the act of running a trained model to produce predictions from input data. In a simplified workflow, predictions are generated and then compared with known targets during evaluation. In practice, evaluation may include validation during training and a separate held-out test set; inference is not necessarily a single mandatory stage between training and every form of evaluation.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":119,"question":"What is the primary role of the vector database in a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To store the large language model (LLM) itself."},{"key":"b","text":"To store and index numerical embeddings of text for efficient similarity search."},{"key":"c","text":"To fine-tune the large language model on new data."},{"key":"d","text":"To store the chat history of user conversations."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A basic RAG pipeline has two essential roles: a retriever searches an external knowledge source for relevant passages, and a generator (usually an LLM) uses those passages plus the user’s question to compose an answer. Fine-tuning is a separate model-adaptation process; grammatical validation alone does not supply missing evidence.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":120,"question":"What is the primary function of the RAG technique in the context of large language models?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A method for fine-tuning a model by updating all of its weights on a new data set."},{"key":"b","text":"A technique that enhances a model's responses by first retrieving relevant information from an external knowledge base."},{"key":"c","text":"A type of hardware accelerator designed to speed up model training."},{"key":"d","text":"A data encryption standard used to protect the model's training data."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A basic RAG pipeline has two essential roles: a retriever searches an external knowledge source for relevant passages, and a generator (usually an LLM) uses those passages plus the user’s question to compose an answer. Fine-tuning is a separate model-adaptation process; grammatical validation alone does not supply missing evidence.","tip":"Exam takeaway: identify the concept’s purpose, what it operates on, and what output or guarantee it provides; then eliminate options describing a different task or workflow stage.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":121,"question":"In the self-attention mechanism, which matrix represents the token seeking relevant information?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Key (K)"},{"key":"b","text":"Value (V)"},{"key":"c","text":"Query (Q)"},{"key":"d","text":"Output (O)"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"In attention mechanism, Query (Q) represents the token that is \"asking\" for information. Key (K) is used to compute compatibility with other tokens, and Value (V) contains the actual information to be retrieved. Think of it as Q=\"what am I looking for?\", K=\"what do I have?\", V=\"the actual content.\"","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":122,"question":"When comparing two LLMs using BLEU score, a higher BLEU indicates:","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The model has a broader vocabulary"},{"key":"b","text":"The model understands complex sentence structures better"},{"key":"c","text":"The model produces text more similar to reference text"},{"key":"d","text":"The model has higher computational efficiency"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"BLEU (Bilingual Evaluation Understudy) measures n-gram overlap between generated text and reference text. Higher BLEU specifically indicates greater lexical similarity to reference, NOT broader vocabulary or deeper understanding. It's a surface-level similarity metric.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":123,"question":"What is the main benefit of model quantization?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Increasing model accuracy"},{"key":"b","text":"Reducing memory usage and inference latency"},{"key":"c","text":"Improving training stability"},{"key":"d","text":"Enhancing model interpretability"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Quantization reduces numerical precision (e.g., from FP32 to INT8), which significantly reduces memory footprint and speeds up inference. This comes with a small trade-off in accuracy but massive gains in efficiency and cost reduction for deployment.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":124,"question":"Which NVIDIA tool is specifically designed for high-performance model inference serving?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NeMo Framework"},{"key":"b","text":"CUDA Toolkit"},{"key":"c","text":"Triton Inference Server"},{"key":"d","text":"TensorRT"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Triton Inference Server is NVIDIA's dedicated platform for deploying AI models at scale in production. It handles multiple frameworks, dynamic batching, model versioning, and monitoring. TensorRT optimizes models, NeMo is for development, CUDA is low-level programming.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":125,"question":"In A/B testing for LLM deployment, what is the recommended traffic distribution approach?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Send all traffic to the new model immediately"},{"key":"b","text":"Deploy both models and serve traffic equally"},{"key":"c","text":"Use only internal testing"},{"key":"d","text":"Gradually increase traffic over months"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Equal (50/50) traffic distribution ensures fair comparison between models and enables statistically significant performance measurement. This approach minimizes bias and provides reliable data for decision-making about which model to fully deploy.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":126,"question":"What is the primary advantage of transfer learning in LLM development?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Eliminates need for labeled data"},{"key":"b","text":"Reduces training time and computational requirements"},{"key":"c","text":"Guarantees better performance"},{"key":"d","text":"Removes all biases from models"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Transfer learning leverages knowledge learned from large-scale pre-training, dramatically reducing the time and computational resources needed for task-specific fine-tuning. It doesn't eliminate the need for labeled data entirely or guarantee performance, but makes training much more efficient.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":127,"question":"Which evaluation metric is most commonly used for text summarization tasks?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"BLEU"},{"key":"b","text":"ROUGE"},{"key":"c","text":"Perplexity"},{"key":"d","text":"F1-Score"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"ROUGE (Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation) is specifically designed for evaluating summarization quality. It measures overlap between generated and reference summaries. BLEU is for translation, perplexity for language modeling, F1 for classification.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":128,"question":"What does masked multi-head attention in BERT enable?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Faster training speed"},{"key":"b","text":"Bidirectional context understanding"},{"key":"c","text":"Reduced memory usage"},{"key":"d","text":"Better long sequence handling"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"BERT's masked attention allows tokens to attend to all other tokens in both directions (past and future), unlike GPT which only attends to previous tokens. This bidirectional context enables better understanding tasks but prevents autoregressive generation.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":129,"question":"What is the main purpose of layer normalization in transformers?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To reduce overfitting"},{"key":"b","text":"To stabilize training and enable deeper networks"},{"key":"c","text":"To compress model size"},{"key":"d","text":"To speed up inference"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Layer normalization normalizes inputs to each layer, reducing internal covariate shift and stabilizing gradients. This enables training of very deep networks (like transformers with many layers) and often leads to faster convergence.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":130,"question":"Which architecture type is GPT based on?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Encoder-only transformer"},{"key":"b","text":"Decoder-only transformer"},{"key":"c","text":"Encoder-decoder transformer"},{"key":"d","text":"Recurrent neural network"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"GPT uses only the decoder part of the transformer, with masked self-attention that prevents looking at future tokens. This autoregressive design enables text generation by predicting the next token based on previous tokens.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":131,"question":"What is the key difference between BERT and GPT training objectives?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"BERT uses autoregressive modeling, GPT uses masked language modeling"},{"key":"b","text":"BERT uses masked language modeling, GPT uses autoregressive modeling"},{"key":"c","text":"Both use the same training objective"},{"key":"d","text":"BERT uses supervised learning, GPT uses unsupervised learning"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"BERT masks random tokens and predicts them using bidirectional context (Masked Language Modeling). GPT predicts the next token given previous tokens (autoregressive). This difference makes BERT better for understanding tasks, GPT better for generation.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":132,"question":"In Named Entity Recognition (NER), what type of information is typically extracted?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Sentiment polarity"},{"key":"b","text":"Topic categories"},{"key":"c","text":"Person, organization, location names"},{"key":"d","text":"Grammar structures"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NER identifies and classifies named entities in text into predefined categories like PERSON (John Smith), ORGANIZATION (Google), LOCATION (New York), DATE, etc. It's a key information extraction task used in search, content analysis, and privacy protection.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":133,"question":"What is the purpose of the attention mask in transformer models?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To hide irrelevant tokens during computation"},{"key":"b","text":"To reduce computational complexity"},{"key":"c","text":"To prevent attention to padding tokens or future tokens"},{"key":"d","text":"To compress attention weights"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Attention masks ensure tokens don't attend to padding tokens (in batched sequences) or future tokens (in autoregressive models like GPT). This maintains the intended information flow and prevents the model from \"cheating\" by seeing future information during training.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":134,"question":"Which NVIDIA framework provides pre-trained models for NLP tasks?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Triton"},{"key":"b","text":"TensorRT"},{"key":"c","text":"NeMo"},{"key":"d","text":"CUDA"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NeMo (Neural Modules) is NVIDIA's framework for building AI applications, providing pre-trained models for NLP, speech, and vision tasks with easy customization and fine-tuning capabilities. Triton serves models, TensorRT optimizes them, CUDA is low-level programming.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":135,"question":"What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A training technique for transformers"},{"key":"b","text":"A method combining retrieval and generation for responses"},{"key":"c","text":"A model compression technique"},{"key":"d","text":"An evaluation metric for language models"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"RAG combines information retrieval with text generation. It first retrieves relevant documents from a knowledge base, then uses that context to generate more accurate, up-to-date responses. This reduces hallucination and enables access to external knowledge beyond training data.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":136,"question":"In multi-head attention, why are multiple attention heads used?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To reduce computational cost"},{"key":"b","text":"To focus on different types of relationships simultaneously"},{"key":"c","text":"To handle longer sequences"},{"key":"d","text":"To improve memory efficiency"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Multiple attention heads allow the model to attend to different types of relationships simultaneously - one head might focus on syntactic relationships, another on semantic similarity, etc. Each head learns different attention patterns, providing richer representations.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":137,"question":"What is the typical range of BLEU scores?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"-1 to 1"},{"key":"b","text":"0 to 100"},{"key":"c","text":"0 to 1"},{"key":"d","text":"1 to 10"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"BLEU scores range from 0 to 1, where 0 means no overlap with reference text and 1 means perfect match. Scores are often reported as percentages (0-100), but the underlying scale is 0-1. Higher scores indicate better similarity to reference text.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":138,"question":"Which technique is most effective for reducing LLM inference costs in production?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Increasing batch size only"},{"key":"b","text":"Using larger models"},{"key":"c","text":"Model quantization and optimization"},{"key":"d","text":"Adding more GPUs"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Model quantization and optimization techniques (like TensorRT) reduce memory usage and computational requirements, directly lowering infrastructure costs. While batch size helps throughput, and more GPUs increase capacity, optimization reduces the fundamental resource requirements per inference.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":139,"question":"What is the main advantage of Parameter-Efficient Fine-tuning (PEFT) methods like LoRA?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Better accuracy than full fine-tuning"},{"key":"b","text":"Reduces computational requirements while maintaining performance"},{"key":"c","text":"Eliminates need for pre-trained models"},{"key":"d","text":"Faster inference speed"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"PEFT methods like LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) fine-tune only a small subset of parameters, dramatically reducing memory and computational requirements while achieving performance comparable to full fine-tuning. This makes fine-tuning accessible with limited resources.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":140,"question":"In transformer architecture, what connects different layers?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Attention mechanisms only"},{"key":"b","text":"Feed-forward networks only"},{"key":"c","text":"Residual connections"},{"key":"d","text":"Pooling layers"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Residual connections (skip connections) add the input of each sub-layer to its output, enabling gradient flow in deep networks and preventing vanishing gradients. They're combined with layer normalization in the \"Add & Norm\" operations throughout the transformer.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":141,"question":"What is the purpose of the softmax function in attention computation?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To normalize attention weights to sum to 1"},{"key":"b","text":"To increase computational speed"},{"key":"c","text":"To reduce memory usage"},{"key":"d","text":"To handle negative values"}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Softmax converts raw attention scores into a probability distribution where all weights sum to 1. This ensures that attention is properly distributed across tokens and creates a weighted average when applied to values. The normalization is essential for stable training.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":142,"question":"Which model format is supported by TensorRT for optimization?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Only PyTorch models"},{"key":"b","text":"Only TensorFlow models"},{"key":"c","text":"Multiple formats including ONNX"},{"key":"d","text":"Only custom NVIDIA formats"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"TensorRT supports multiple model formats including ONNX (Open Neural Network Exchange), TensorFlow, PyTorch, and others. ONNX is particularly important as it enables model portability across different frameworks and deployment platforms.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":143,"question":"What is the key benefit of dynamic batching in Triton Inference Server?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Reduces model size"},{"key":"b","text":"Optimizes throughput by combining requests"},{"key":"c","text":"Improves model accuracy"},{"key":"d","text":"Reduces latency for all requests"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Dynamic batching automatically combines multiple incoming requests into batches to maximize GPU utilization and overall throughput. While individual request latency might increase slightly, the system can handle many more requests per second.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":144,"question":"In text classification tasks, what does F1-score measure?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Only precision"},{"key":"b","text":"Only recall"},{"key":"c","text":"Harmonic mean of precision and recall"},{"key":"d","text":"Arithmetic mean of precision and recall"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"F1-score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall: F1 = 2 x (precision x recall) / (precision + recall). It provides a single metric that balances both precision (correct positive predictions) and recall (finding all positive cases), especially useful for imbalanced datasets.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":145,"question":"Which training paradigm involves learning from massive unlabeled text corpora?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Supervised learning"},{"key":"b","text":"Pre-training"},{"key":"c","text":"Fine-tuning"},{"key":"d","text":"Transfer learning"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Pre-training involves learning general language representations from large-scale unlabeled text (like web pages, books) using self-supervised objectives like predicting masked tokens or next tokens. This creates foundation models that can then be fine-tuned for specific tasks.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":146,"question":"What is the purpose of teacher forcing in sequence generation?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To speed up inference"},{"key":"b","text":"To use ground truth tokens during training"},{"key":"c","text":"To reduce model size"},{"key":"d","text":"To improve model interpretability"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Teacher forcing feeds the ground truth token at each time step during training instead of the model's own prediction. This stabilizes training and speeds convergence, though it can create a mismatch between training and inference (exposure bias).","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":147,"question":"In question answering tasks, what distinguishes extractive from generative approaches?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Model architecture used"},{"key":"b","text":"Whether answers are extracted from text or generated"},{"key":"c","text":"Training data requirements"},{"key":"d","text":"Computational complexity"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Extractive QA finds and extracts the answer span from the given passage (like highlighting text). Generative QA creates new text as the answer, potentially combining information from multiple sources or paraphrasing. Both can use similar architectures but have different output strategies.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":148,"question":"What is the main advantage of using pre-trained embeddings?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Faster training speed only"},{"key":"b","text":"Capturing semantic relationships learned from large corpora"},{"key":"c","text":"Reduced memory usage"},{"key":"d","text":"Better interpretability"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Pre-trained embeddings (like Word2Vec, GloVe) capture semantic relationships between words learned from large text corpora. Words with similar meanings have similar embeddings, providing rich representations that improve downstream task performance, especially with limited training data.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":149,"question":"In the context of LLMs, what does \"few-shot learning\" refer to?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Training with very few parameters"},{"key":"b","text":"Learning tasks with minimal examples"},{"key":"c","text":"Fast inference speed"},{"key":"d","text":"Short sequence processing"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Few-shot learning enables LLMs to perform new tasks by providing just a few examples in the input prompt, without additional training or fine-tuning. For example, showing 2-3 examples of sentiment classification in the prompt to classify new text.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":150,"question":"What is the role of the feed-forward network in each transformer layer?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To compute attention weights"},{"key":"b","text":"To apply non-linear transformations to representations"},{"key":"c","text":"To normalize layer outputs"},{"key":"d","text":"To handle positional information"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"The feed-forward network applies non-linear transformations (typically two linear layers with ReLU activation) to each position independently. This adds modeling capacity and non-linearity to the otherwise linear attention operations, enabling complex pattern learning.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":151,"question":"What is the purpose of beam search in sequence generation?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To speed up generation"},{"key":"b","text":"To explore multiple candidate sequences simultaneously"},{"key":"c","text":"To reduce memory usage"},{"key":"d","text":"To handle longer sequences"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Beam search maintains multiple candidate sequences (beams) during generation, exploring different paths to find higher-quality outputs. It balances between greedy search (fast but potentially suboptimal) and exhaustive search (optimal but computationally prohibitive).","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":152,"question":"In distributed training, what is the purpose of gradient accumulation?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To speed up training"},{"key":"b","text":"To simulate larger batch sizes with limited memory"},{"key":"c","text":"To improve model accuracy"},{"key":"d","text":"To reduce communication overhead"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Gradient accumulation allows training with larger effective batch sizes when memory is limited. Instead of updating parameters after each small batch, gradients are accumulated over multiple mini-batches before the parameter update, simulating a larger batch.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":153,"question":"What is the main difference between autoregressive and autoencoding language models?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Model size"},{"key":"b","text":"Training data requirements"},{"key":"c","text":"Generation vs. understanding focus"},{"key":"d","text":"Computational complexity"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Autoregressive models (like GPT) predict the next token sequentially, making them excellent for generation tasks. Autoencoding models (like BERT) use bidirectional context to understand masked tokens, making them better for understanding tasks like classification and question answering.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":154,"question":"Which NVIDIA technology is specifically designed for inference optimization?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NeMo"},{"key":"b","text":"TensorRT"},{"key":"c","text":"DGX"},{"key":"d","text":"CUDA"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"TensorRT is NVIDIA's inference optimization library that accelerates deep learning models through techniques like layer fusion, precision calibration (INT8/FP16), and kernel auto-tuning. It specifically focuses on making inference faster and more efficient.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":155,"question":"In the context of attention mechanisms, what does \"scaled\" refer to in scaled dot-product attention?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Scaling by sequence length"},{"key":"b","text":"Scaling by square root of key dimension"},{"key":"c","text":"Scaling by number of heads"},{"key":"d","text":"Scaling by batch size"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"The attention scores (QK transposed) are divided by the square root of d_k (the key dimension) to prevent extremely large values that would cause the softmax to have very small gradients. This scaling ensures stable training.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":156,"question":"What is the main advantage of using ONNX format for models?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Better accuracy"},{"key":"b","text":"Faster training"},{"key":"c","text":"Model portability across frameworks"},{"key":"d","text":"Reduced model size"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"ONNX (Open Neural Network Exchange) is a standard format that enables models trained in one framework (PyTorch, TensorFlow, etc.) to be deployed in another framework or runtime. This portability simplifies deployment and enables optimization tools like TensorRT.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":157,"question":"In fine-tuning scenarios, what is catastrophic forgetting?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Model losing pre-trained knowledge during fine-tuning"},{"key":"b","text":"Model forgetting current task"},{"key":"c","text":"Memory overflow during training"},{"key":"d","text":"Gradient explosion problem"}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Catastrophic forgetting occurs when fine-tuning on new tasks causes the model to lose previously learned knowledge from pre-training or other tasks. This is why techniques like parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) and careful learning rate scheduling are important.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":158,"question":"What is the purpose of nucleus sampling (top-p) in text generation?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To speed up generation"},{"key":"b","text":"To control randomness by sampling from top probability mass"},{"key":"c","text":"To reduce memory usage"},{"key":"d","text":"To handle longer sequences"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Nucleus sampling (top-p) samples from the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability exceeds p (e.g., 0.9). This dynamically adjusts the candidate pool size based on the probability distribution, balancing diversity and quality better than fixed top-k sampling.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":159,"question":"Which component of BERT is responsible for handling different sentence pairs?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Multi-head attention"},{"key":"b","text":"Positional encoding"},{"key":"c","text":"Segment embeddings"},{"key":"d","text":"Layer normalization"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Segment embeddings (also called token type embeddings) distinguish between different sentences in tasks like Next Sentence Prediction. They add learned embeddings to indicate whether each token belongs to sentence A or sentence B in sentence pair tasks.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":160,"question":"What is the main purpose of knowledge distillation in model compression?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To transfer knowledge from larger to smaller models"},{"key":"b","text":"To improve training speed"},{"key":"c","text":"To reduce inference latency"},{"key":"d","text":"To handle longer sequences"}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Knowledge distillation trains a smaller \"student\" model to mimic the behavior of a larger \"teacher\" model by learning from the teacher's soft outputs (probability distributions) rather than just hard labels. This creates compact models with better performance than training from scratch.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":161,"question":"In the context of LLMs, what does \"hallucination\" refer to?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Model generating factually incorrect information"},{"key":"b","text":"Model processing images"},{"key":"c","text":"Model compression artifacts"},{"key":"d","text":"Training instability"}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Hallucination occurs when LLMs generate plausible-sounding but factually incorrect information. This happens because models learn patterns from training data but may not truly \"understand\" facts, leading to confident-sounding but false statements.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":162,"question":"What is the key innovation of the Transformer architecture over RNNs?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Better memory efficiency"},{"key":"b","text":"Parallel processing capability"},{"key":"c","text":"Smaller model size"},{"key":"d","text":"Faster inference only"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Transformers can process all tokens in a sequence simultaneously (parallel processing), unlike RNNs which must process tokens sequentially. This parallelization enables much faster training and better utilization of modern hardware like GPUs.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":163,"question":"Which technique is commonly used to handle out-of-vocabulary words in modern NLP?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Word-level tokenization only"},{"key":"b","text":"Subword tokenization (BPE, WordPiece)"},{"key":"c","text":"Character-level processing only"},{"key":"d","text":"Dictionary lookup"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Subword tokenization (like BPE - Byte Pair Encoding, WordPiece) breaks words into smaller subword units, allowing models to handle unseen words by combining known subword pieces. This reduces vocabulary size while maintaining semantic meaning.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":164,"question":"What is the purpose of warm-up in learning rate scheduling?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To speed up training"},{"key":"b","text":"To gradually increase learning rate at training start"},{"key":"c","text":"To reduce memory usage"},{"key":"d","text":"To prevent overfitting"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Learning rate warm-up gradually increases the learning rate from a small value to the target value over the first few epochs/steps. This helps stabilize training of large models by preventing large gradient updates when parameters are randomly initialized.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":165,"question":"In production LLM deployment, what is the most critical consideration for cost optimization?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Model accuracy only"},{"key":"b","text":"Balancing performance, latency, and computational costs"},{"key":"c","text":"Using the largest available model"},{"key":"d","text":"Maximum throughput regardless of cost"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Production deployment requires balancing multiple factors: model performance (accuracy), latency requirements (user experience), and computational costs (infrastructure expenses). The optimal solution rarely maximizes just one dimension but finds the best trade-off for business requirements.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":166,"question":"How does A/B testing contribute to the optimization of deep learning models' performance and effectiveness in real-world applications? (Pick the 2 correct responses)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A/B testing helps validate the impact of changes or updates to deep learning models by statistically analyzing the outcomes of different versions to make informed decisions for model optimization."},{"key":"b","text":"A/B testing allows for the comparison of different model configurations or hyperparameters to identify the most effective setup for improved performance."},{"key":"c","text":"A/B testing in deep learning models is primarily used for selecting the best training dataset without requiring a model architecture or parameters."},{"key":"d","text":"A/B testing guarantees immediate performance improvements in deep learning models without the need for further analysis or experimentation."},{"key":"e","text":"A/B testing is irrelevant in deep learning as it only applies to traditional statistical analysis and not complex neural network models."}],"answers":["a","b"],"multi":true,"explanation":"A/B testing is a controlled experimentation technique used to compare two versions of a system to determine which performs better. In the context of deep learning, it validates changes (e.g., model updates or new features) by statistically comparing outcomes (e.g., accuracy or user engagement), enabling data-driven optimization decisions. It also allows comparison of different model configurations or hyperparameters to identify the most effective setup.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":167,"question":"In neural networks, the vanishing gradient problem refers to what problem or issue?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The problem of overfitting in neural networks, where the model performs well on the training data but poorly on new, unseen data."},{"key":"b","text":"The issue of gradients becoming too large during backpropagation, leading to unstable training."},{"key":"c","text":"The problem of underfitting in neural networks, where the model fails to capture the underlying patterns in the data."},{"key":"d","text":"The issue of gradients becoming too small during backpropagation, resulting in slow convergence or stagnation of the training process."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"The vanishing gradient problem occurs in deep neural networks when gradients become too small during backpropagation, causing slow convergence or stagnation in training, particularly in deeper layers. This issue is common in architectures like RNNs or deep feedforward networks with certain activation functions (e.g., sigmoid). Techniques like ReLU activation, batch normalization, or residual connections (used in transformers) mitigate this problem.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":168,"question":"In the context of fine-tuning LLMs, which of the following metrics is most commonly used to assess the performance of a fine-tuned model?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Model size"},{"key":"b","text":"Accuracy on a validation set"},{"key":"c","text":"Training duration"},{"key":"d","text":"Number of layers"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"When fine-tuning large language models, the primary goal is to improve the model's performance on a specific task. The most common metric for assessing this performance is accuracy on a validation set, as it directly measures how well the model generalizes to unseen data. Options like model size, training duration, and number of layers are architectural characteristics or training parameters that do not directly reflect effectiveness.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":169,"question":"Which of the following is a key characteristic of Rapid Application Development (RAD)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Iterative prototyping with active user involvement."},{"key":"b","text":"Extensive upfront planning before any development."},{"key":"c","text":"Linear progression through predefined project phases."},{"key":"d","text":"Minimal user feedback during the development process."}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Rapid Application Development (RAD) is a software development methodology that emphasizes iterative prototyping and active user involvement to accelerate development and ensure alignment with user needs. RAD involves creating prototypes, gathering user feedback, and refining the application iteratively, unlike traditional waterfall models that require extensive upfront planning.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":170,"question":"Which Python library is specifically designed for working with large language models (LLMs)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NumPy"},{"key":"b","text":"Pandas"},{"key":"c","text":"HuggingFace Transformers"},{"key":"d","text":"Scikit-learn"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"The HuggingFace Transformers library is specifically designed for working with large language models, providing tools for model training, fine-tuning, and inference with transformer-based architectures (e.g., BERT, GPT, T5). NumPy is for numerical computations, Pandas is for data manipulation, and Scikit-learn is for traditional machine learning.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":171,"question":"What is the primary purpose of applying various image transformation techniques (e.g., flipping, rotation, zooming) to a dataset?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To simplify the model's architecture, making it easier to interpret the results."},{"key":"b","text":"To artificially expand the dataset's size and improve the model's ability to generalize."},{"key":"c","text":"To ensure perfect alignment and uniformity across all images in the dataset."},{"key":"d","text":"To reduce the computational resources required for training deep learning models."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Image transformation techniques such as flipping, rotation, and zooming are forms of data augmentation used to artificially increase the size and diversity of a dataset. Data augmentation improves a model's ability to generalize by exposing it to varied versions of the training data, thus reducing overfitting. Transformations do not simplify architecture, introduce variability rather than uniformity, and typically increase computational requirements.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":172,"question":"Which feature of the HuggingFace Transformers library makes it particularly suitable for fine-tuning large language models on NVIDIA GPUs?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Built-in support for CPU-based data preprocessing pipelines."},{"key":"b","text":"Seamless integration with PyTorch and TensorRT for GPU-accelerated training and inference."},{"key":"c","text":"Automatic conversion of models to ONNX format for cross-platform deployment."},{"key":"d","text":"Simplified API for classical machine learning algorithms like SVM."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"The HuggingFace Transformers library is widely used for fine-tuning large language models due to its seamless integration with PyTorch and NVIDIA's TensorRT, enabling GPU-accelerated training and inference. This includes support for CUDA and features like mixed-precision training and dynamic shape inference, making it ideal for scaling LLM fine-tuning on GPU clusters.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":173,"question":"When designing prompts for a large language model to perform a complex reasoning task, such as solving a multi-step mathematical problem, which advanced prompt engineering technique is most effective in ensuring robust performance across diverse inputs?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Zero-shot prompting with a generic task description."},{"key":"b","text":"Few-shot prompting with randomly selected examples."},{"key":"c","text":"Chain-of-thought prompting with step-by-step reasoning examples."},{"key":"d","text":"Retrieval-augmented generation with external mathematical databases."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting is an advanced prompt engineering technique that significantly enhances a large language model's performance on complex reasoning tasks. By including examples that explicitly demonstrate step-by-step reasoning in the prompt, CoT guides the model to break down the problem into intermediate steps, improving accuracy and robustness. Zero-shot is less effective for complex tasks, few-shot with random examples is suboptimal without structured reasoning, and RAG is useful for factual queries but less relevant for pure reasoning tasks.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":174,"question":"In the development of trustworthy AI systems, what is the primary purpose of implementing red-teaming exercises during the alignment process of large language models?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To optimize the model's inference speed for production deployment."},{"key":"b","text":"To identify and mitigate potential biases, safety risks, and harmful outputs."},{"key":"c","text":"To increase the model's parameter count for better performance."},{"key":"d","text":"To automate the collection of training data for fine-tuning."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Red-teaming exercises involve systematically testing a large language model by probing it with adversarial or challenging inputs to uncover vulnerabilities, such as biases, unsafe responses, or harmful outputs. Red-teaming is a critical step in the alignment process to ensure LLMs adhere to ethical standards and societal values. By simulating worst-case scenarios, it helps developers identify and mitigate risks before deployment.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":175,"question":"In the context of preparing a multilingual dataset for fine-tuning an LLM, which preprocessing technique is most effective for handling text from diverse scripts (e.g., Latin, Cyrillic, Devanagari) to ensure consistent model performance?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Normalizing all text to a single script using transliteration."},{"key":"b","text":"Applying Unicode normalization to standardize character encodings."},{"key":"c","text":"Removing all non-Latin characters to simplify the input."},{"key":"d","text":"Converting text to phonetic representations for cross-lingual alignment."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Applying Unicode normalization (e.g., NFKC or NFC forms) is the most effective preprocessing technique for handling text from diverse scripts. Unicode normalization standardizes character encodings, ensuring that visually identical characters (e.g., precomposed vs. decomposed forms) are represented consistently, which improves model performance across languages. Transliteration may lose linguistic nuances, removing non-Latin characters discards critical information, and phonetic conversion is impractical for text-based LLMs.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":176,"question":"Which technique is used in prompt engineering to guide LLMs in generating more accurate and contextually appropriate responses?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Training the model with additional data."},{"key":"b","text":"Choosing another model architecture."},{"key":"c","text":"Increasing the model's parameter count."},{"key":"d","text":"Leveraging the system message."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Prompt engineering involves designing inputs to guide large language models to produce desired outputs without modifying the model itself. Leveraging the system message is a key technique, where a predefined instruction or context is provided to the LLM to set the tone, role, or constraints for its responses. Training with additional data, changing architecture, and increasing parameters involve model modifications, which are not part of prompt engineering.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":177,"question":"When designing an experiment to compare the performance of two LLMs on a question-answering task, which statistical test is most appropriate to determine if the difference in their accuracy is significant, assuming the data follows a normal distribution?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Chi-squared test"},{"key":"b","text":"Paired t-test"},{"key":"c","text":"Mann-Whitney U test"},{"key":"d","text":"ANOVA test"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"The paired t-test is the most appropriate statistical test to compare the performance (e.g., accuracy) of two large language models on the same question-answering dataset, assuming the data follows a normal distribution. This test evaluates whether the mean difference in paired observations is statistically significant. Chi-squared is for categorical data, Mann-Whitney U is for non-normal data, and ANOVA is for comparing more than two groups.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":178,"question":"Which of the following contributes to the ability of RAPIDS to accelerate data processing? (Pick the 2 correct responses)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Ensuring that CPUs are running at full clock speed."},{"key":"b","text":"Subsampling datasets to provide rapid but approximate answers."},{"key":"c","text":"Using the GPU for parallel processing of data."},{"key":"d","text":"Enabling data processing to scale to multiple GPUs."},{"key":"e","text":"Providing more memory for data analysis."}],"answers":["c","d"],"multi":true,"explanation":"RAPIDS is an open-source suite of GPU-accelerated data science libraries developed by NVIDIA to speed up data processing and machine learning workflows. Its key advantages include using GPUs for parallel processing, which significantly accelerates computations compared to CPU-based processing, and enabling data processing to scale to multiple GPUs for even greater throughput.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":179,"question":"Which calculation is most commonly used to measure the semantic closeness of two text passages?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Hamming distance"},{"key":"b","text":"Jaccard similarity"},{"key":"c","text":"Cosine similarity"},{"key":"d","text":"Euclidean distance"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Cosine similarity is the most commonly used metric to measure the semantic closeness of two text passages in NLP. It calculates the cosine of the angle between two vectors (e.g., word embeddings or sentence embeddings) in a high-dimensional space, focusing on direction rather than magnitude. Hamming distance is for binary data, Jaccard similarity is for set-based comparisons, and Euclidean distance is less common for text due to its sensitivity to vector magnitude.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":180,"question":"When preprocessing text data for an LLM fine-tuning task, why is it critical to apply subword tokenization (e.g., Byte-Pair Encoding) instead of word-based tokenization for handling rare or out-of-vocabulary words?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Subword tokenization reduces the model's computational complexity by eliminating embeddings."},{"key":"b","text":"Subword tokenization creates a fixed-size vocabulary to prevent memory overflow."},{"key":"c","text":"Subword tokenization breaks words into smaller units, enabling the model to generalize to unseen words."},{"key":"d","text":"Subword tokenization removes punctuation and special characters to simplify text input."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Subword tokenization, such as Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE) or WordPiece, is critical for preprocessing text data because it breaks words into smaller subword units, enabling the model to handle rare or out-of-vocabulary words effectively. This allows the model to represent unseen words by combining known subwords (e.g., \"unseen\" as \"un\" + \"seen\"), improving generalization compared to word-based tokenization.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":181,"question":"What are the main advantages of instructed large language models over traditional, small language models (< 300M parameters)? (Pick the 2 correct responses)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Trained without the need for labeled data."},{"key":"b","text":"Smaller latency, higher throughput."},{"key":"c","text":"It is easier to explain the predictions."},{"key":"d","text":"Cheaper computational costs during inference."},{"key":"e","text":"Single generic model can do more than one task."}],"answers":["d","e"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Instructed large language models have significant advantages over smaller traditional models. They often have cheaper computational costs during inference for certain tasks because they can generalize across multiple tasks without requiring task-specific retraining. Additionally, a single generic model can perform more than one task, reducing the need for multiple specialized models.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":182,"question":"Which aspect in the development of ethical AI systems ensures they align with societal values and norms?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Achieving the highest possible level of prediction accuracy in AI models."},{"key":"b","text":"Implementing complex algorithms to enhance AI's problem-solving capabilities."},{"key":"c","text":"Developing AI systems with autonomy from human decision-making."},{"key":"d","text":"Ensuring AI systems have explicable decision-making processes."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Ensuring explicable decision-making processes, often referred to as explainability or interpretability, is critical for aligning AI systems with societal values and norms. Explainable AI allows stakeholders to understand how decisions are made, fostering trust and ensuring compliance with ethical standards. Prediction accuracy alone does not guarantee ethical alignment, and autonomy from human oversight can conflict with ethical principles.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":183,"question":"What is the fundamental role of LangChain in an LLM workflow?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To act as a replacement for traditional programming languages."},{"key":"b","text":"To reduce the size of AI foundation models."},{"key":"c","text":"To orchestrate LLM components into complex workflows."},{"key":"d","text":"To directly manage the hardware resources used by LLMs."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"LangChain is a framework designed to simplify the development of applications powered by large language models by orchestrating various components, such as LLMs, external data sources, memory, and tools, into cohesive workflows. It enables developers to build complex applications by chaining together prompts, retrieval systems (e.g., for RAG), and memory modules to maintain context across interactions.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":184,"question":"What distinguishes BLEU scores from ROUGE scores when evaluating natural language processing models?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"BLEU scores determine the fluency of text generation, while ROUGE scores rate the uniqueness of generated text."},{"key":"b","text":"BLEU scores analyze syntactic structures, while ROUGE scores evaluate semantic accuracy."},{"key":"c","text":"BLEU scores evaluate the precision of translations, while ROUGE scores focus on the recall of summarized text."},{"key":"d","text":"BLEU scores measure model efficiency, whereas ROUGE scores assess computational complexity."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"BLEU primarily measures the precision of n-gram overlaps between generated and reference translations, making it suitable for assessing translation quality. ROUGE focuses on recall, measuring the overlap of n-grams, longest common subsequences, or skip-bigrams between generated and reference summaries, making it ideal for summarization tasks. Neither metric evaluates fluency, uniqueness, syntax, semantics, efficiency, or complexity directly.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":185,"question":"What are some methods to overcome limited throughput between CPU and GPU? (Pick the 2 correct responses)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Increase the clock speed of the CPU."},{"key":"b","text":"Using techniques like memory pooling."},{"key":"c","text":"Upgrade the GPU to a higher-end model."},{"key":"d","text":"Increase the number of CPU cores."}],"answers":["b","c"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Limited throughput between CPU and GPU often results from data transfer bottlenecks or inefficient resource utilization. Memory pooling techniques, such as pinned memory or unified memory, reduce data transfer overhead by optimizing how data is staged between CPU and GPU. Upgrading to a higher-end GPU provides more bandwidth and processing power to alleviate the bottleneck.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":186,"question":"What is 'chunking' in Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Rewrite blocks of text to fill a context window."},{"key":"b","text":"A method used in RAG to generate random text."},{"key":"c","text":"A concept in RAG that refers to the training of large language models."},{"key":"d","text":"A technique used in RAG to split text into meaningful segments."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Chunking in Retrieval-Augmented Generation refers to the process of splitting large text documents into smaller, meaningful segments (or chunks) to facilitate efficient retrieval and processing by the LLM. Chunking ensures that retrieved text fits within the model's context window and is relevant to the query, improving the quality of generated responses.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":187,"question":"When using NVIDIA RAPIDS to accelerate data preprocessing for an LLM fine-tuning pipeline, which specific feature of RAPIDS cuDF enables faster data manipulation compared to traditional CPU-based Pandas?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Automatic parallelization of Python code across CPU cores."},{"key":"b","text":"GPU-accelerated columnar data processing with zero-copy memory access."},{"key":"c","text":"Integration with cloud-based storage for distributed data access."},{"key":"d","text":"Conversion of Pandas DataFrames to SQL tables for faster querying."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NVIDIA RAPIDS cuDF is a GPU-accelerated library that mimics Pandas' API but performs data manipulation on GPUs, significantly speeding up preprocessing tasks for LLM fine-tuning. The key feature is GPU-accelerated columnar data processing with zero-copy memory access, which leverages the parallel processing power of GPUs and avoids unnecessary data transfers between CPU and GPU memory.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":188,"question":"In the context of developing an AI application using NVIDIA's NGC containers, how does the use of containerized environments enhance the reproducibility of LLM training and deployment workflows?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Containers automatically optimize the model's hyperparameters for better performance."},{"key":"b","text":"Containers encapsulate dependencies and configurations, ensuring consistent execution across systems."},{"key":"c","text":"Containers reduce the model's memory footprint by compressing the neural network."},{"key":"d","text":"Containers enable direct access to GPU hardware without driver installation."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NVIDIA's NGC containers provide pre-configured environments for AI workloads, enhancing reproducibility by encapsulating dependencies, libraries, and configurations. Containers ensure that LLM training and deployment workflows run consistently across different systems by isolating the environment from host system variations. Containers do not optimize hyperparameters, compress models, or eliminate the need for GPU drivers.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":189,"question":"What type of model would you use in emotion classification tasks?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Auto-encoder model"},{"key":"b","text":"Siamese model"},{"key":"c","text":"Encoder model"},{"key":"d","text":"SVM model"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Encoder models, such as those based on transformer architectures (e.g., BERT, RoBERTa), are well-suited for emotion classification tasks because they generate contextualized representations of input text, capturing semantic and syntactic information for downstream classification. Auto-encoders are for unsupervised learning or reconstruction, Siamese models are for similarity tasks, and SVMs are traditional ML models less effective than modern encoders for NLP.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":190,"question":"What is a Tokenizer in Large Language Models (LLM)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A method to remove stop words and punctuation marks from text data."},{"key":"b","text":"A machine learning algorithm that predicts the next word/token in a sequence of text."},{"key":"c","text":"A tool used to split text into smaller units called tokens for analysis and processing."},{"key":"d","text":"A technique used to convert text data into numerical representations called tokens for machine learning."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A tokenizer in the context of large language models is a tool that splits text into smaller units called tokens (e.g., words, subwords, or characters) for processing by the model. Algorithms like WordPiece, Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE), or SentencePiece break text into manageable units to handle vocabulary constraints and out-of-vocabulary words. Removing stop words is a separate preprocessing step, and converting text to numerical representations is the role of embeddings.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":191,"question":"In the context of machine learning model deployment, how can Docker be utilized to enhance the process?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To automatically generate features for machine learning models."},{"key":"b","text":"To provide a consistent environment for model training and inference."},{"key":"c","text":"To reduce the computational resources needed for training models."},{"key":"d","text":"To directly increase the accuracy of machine learning models."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Docker is a containerization platform that ensures consistent environments for machine learning model training and inference by packaging dependencies, libraries, and configurations into portable containers. This eliminates environment discrepancies between development and production, ensuring reproducibility. Docker does not generate features, reduce computational requirements, or affect model accuracy.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":192,"question":"In the context of a natural language processing application, which approach is most effective for implementing zero-shot learning to classify text data into categories that were not seen during training?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Use rule-based systems to manually define the characteristics of each category."},{"key":"b","text":"Use a large, labeled dataset for each possible category."},{"key":"c","text":"Train the new model from scratch for each new category encountered."},{"key":"d","text":"Use a pre-trained language model with semantic embeddings."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Pre-trained language models (e.g., BERT, GPT) with semantic embeddings are highly effective for zero-shot learning because they encode general linguistic knowledge and can generalize to new tasks by leveraging semantic similarity. Rule-based systems lack scalability, using labeled data contradicts zero-shot learning, and training from scratch is impractical and defeats the purpose.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":193,"question":"When comparing and contrasting the ReLU and sigmoid activation functions, which statement is true?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"ReLU is a linear function while sigmoid is non-linear."},{"key":"b","text":"ReLU is less computationally efficient than sigmoid, but it is more accurate than sigmoid."},{"key":"c","text":"ReLU and sigmoid both have a range of 0 to 1."},{"key":"d","text":"ReLU is more computationally efficient, but sigmoid is better for predicting probabilities."}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit), defined as f(x) = max(0, x), is computationally efficient because it involves simple thresholding, avoiding expensive exponential calculations required by sigmoid, f(x) = 1/(1 + e^(-x)). Sigmoid outputs values in the range [0, 1], making it suitable for predicting probabilities in binary classification tasks. ReLU has an unbounded positive range [0, infinity), making it less suited for direct probability prediction but effective at mitigating vanishing gradient issues. ReLU is non-linear (piecewise linear), not linear.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":194,"question":"Transformers are useful for language modeling because their architecture is uniquely suited for handling which of the following?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Long sequences"},{"key":"b","text":"Embeddings"},{"key":"c","text":"Class tokens"},{"key":"d","text":"Translations"}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"The transformer architecture is particularly effective for language modeling due to its ability to handle long sequences. Unlike RNNs, which struggle with long-term dependencies due to sequential processing, transformers use self-attention mechanisms to process all tokens simultaneously, capturing relationships across long distances. Embeddings are a component, class tokens are specific to certain models like BERT, and translations are an application rather than a structural advantage.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":195,"question":"You have access to training data but no access to test data. What evaluation method can you use to assess the performance of your AI model?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Cross-validation"},{"key":"b","text":"Randomized controlled trial"},{"key":"c","text":"Average entropy approximation"},{"key":"d","text":"Greedy decoding"}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"When test data is unavailable, cross-validation is the most effective method to assess an AI model's performance using only the training dataset. Cross-validation involves splitting the training data into multiple subsets (folds), training the model on some folds, and validating it on others, repeating this process to estimate generalization performance. Randomized controlled trials are clinical methods, average entropy approximation is not a standard evaluation method, and greedy decoding is a generation strategy.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":196,"question":"Which principle of Trustworthy AI primarily concerns the ethical implications of AI's impact on society and includes considerations for both potential misuse and unintended consequences?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Certification"},{"key":"b","text":"Data Privacy"},{"key":"c","text":"Accountability"},{"key":"d","text":"Legal Responsibility"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Accountability is a core principle of Trustworthy AI that addresses the ethical implications of AI's societal impact, including potential misuse and unintended consequences. It ensures that AI systems are transparent, responsible, and answerable for their outcomes, including mitigating risks of bias and ensuring fairness. Certification refers to compliance processes, Data Privacy focuses on protecting user data, and Legal Responsibility is related but narrower in scope.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":197,"question":"Which tool would you use to select training data with specific keywords?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"ActionScript"},{"key":"b","text":"Tableau dashboard"},{"key":"c","text":"JSON parser"},{"key":"d","text":"Regular expression filter"}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Regular expression (regex) filters are widely used in data preprocessing to select text data containing specific keywords or patterns. A regex pattern can efficiently filter datasets based on textual criteria, enabling targeted data curation. ActionScript is a multimedia programming language, Tableau is for visualization, and JSON parsers handle structured data rather than keyword-based text selection.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":198,"question":"Which of the following claims is correct about quantization in the context of Deep Learning? (Pick the 2 correct responses)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Quantization might help in saving power and reducing heat production."},{"key":"b","text":"It consists of removing a quantity of weights whose values are zero."},{"key":"c","text":"It leads to a substantial loss of model accuracy."},{"key":"d","text":"Helps reduce memory requirements and achieve better cache utilization."},{"key":"e","text":"It only involves reducing the number of bits of the parameters."}],"answers":["a","d"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Quantization in deep learning involves reducing the precision of model weights and activations (e.g., from 32-bit floating-point to 8-bit integers) to optimize performance. It reduces power consumption and heat production by lowering computational intensity, and it helps reduce memory requirements while achieving better cache utilization. Removing zero-valued weights is pruning, not quantization. Quantization typically causes only a small accuracy loss, not a substantial one.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":199,"question":"In the context of evaluating a fine-tuned LLM for a text classification task, which experimental design technique ensures robust performance estimation when dealing with imbalanced datasets?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Single hold-out validation with a fixed test set."},{"key":"b","text":"Stratified k-fold cross-validation."},{"key":"c","text":"Bootstrapping with random sampling."},{"key":"d","text":"Grid search for hyperparameter tuning."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Stratified k-fold cross-validation is a robust experimental design technique for evaluating machine learning models, especially on imbalanced datasets. It divides the dataset into k folds while preserving the class distribution in each fold, ensuring that the model is evaluated on representative samples of all classes. Single hold-out validation is less robust, bootstrapping introduces variability, and grid search is for hyperparameter tuning rather than performance estimation.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":200,"question":"You are working on a fraud detection model for a financial institution. The dataset contains 10,000 transactions, with only 2% labeled as fraudulent. Which technique would be most effective for handling this class imbalance?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Increase the learning rate to prioritize minority class learning."},{"key":"b","text":"Apply SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique) to generate synthetic fraudulent transaction examples."},{"key":"c","text":"Use a larger batch size during training to ensure both classes are represented."},{"key":"d","text":"Reduce the number of features in the dataset to simplify the model."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"SMOTE generates synthetic examples of the minority class (fraudulent transactions) by interpolating between existing minority samples, creating a more balanced dataset without losing information. Increasing learning rate, batch size, or reducing features do not directly address the core problem of class imbalance.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":201,"question":"You are preparing a dataset to train a neural network. The features in your dataset have different scales: one feature ranges from 0-1, another from 0-1000. Which NumPy function would be most appropriate to normalize these features?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"np.random.shuffle()"},{"key":"b","text":"np.linalg.norm()"},{"key":"c","text":"np.interp()"},{"key":"d","text":"np.clip()"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"np.linalg.norm() computes vector norms, which can be used to normalize feature vectors so all features fall on a comparable scale. np.random.shuffle() randomizes order, np.interp() interpolates values, and np.clip() truncates values to a range but doesn't normalize them.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":202,"question":"As a data scientist analyzing a transformer model's behavior, you need to visualize the attention weights across different heads. Which visualization would best help you understand which tokens attend to each other?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Bar chart of average attention weights."},{"key":"b","text":"Scatter plot of Query vs Key vectors."},{"key":"c","text":"Heatmap showing attention scores between all token pairs for each head."},{"key":"d","text":"Line graph of attention weight changes across layers."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A heatmap of attention scores between all token pairs provides the most comprehensive view of which tokens the model focuses on when processing each token. It reveals patterns like syntactic or semantic relationships across different attention heads. Bar charts, scatter plots, and line graphs offer less detailed views of token-to-token attention dynamics.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":203,"question":"You need to develop a multilingual customer support chatbot that can understand queries in English, Spanish, and Mandarin. Which model selection approach would be most effective?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Train separate monolingual models for each language and route queries accordingly."},{"key":"b","text":"Use a pre-trained multilingual model like mBERT or XLM-RoBERTa and fine-tune on your multilingual support data."},{"key":"c","text":"Train a single English model and use machine translation for other languages."},{"key":"d","text":"Use a rule-based system with multilingual dictionaries."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Pre-trained multilingual models like mBERT or XLM-RoBERTa have been trained on text from many languages and capture cross-lingual representations. Fine-tuning on domain-specific multilingual data is the most effective approach, as it leverages shared linguistic knowledge across languages while adapting to the specific customer support domain.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":204,"question":"You're working with a large e-commerce dataset containing over 50 million transaction records. You need to perform complex joins and aggregations. Which NVIDIA RAPIDS component would best accelerate this task?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"cuML for machine learning operations."},{"key":"b","text":"cuGraph for graph analytics."},{"key":"c","text":"cuDF for GPU-accelerated dataframe operations."},{"key":"d","text":"cuSignal for signal processing."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"cuDF is the RAPIDS component specifically designed for GPU-accelerated dataframe operations, including complex joins and aggregations on large datasets. It provides a Pandas-like API accelerated by CUDA for massive throughput. cuML handles ML algorithms, cuGraph handles graph analytics, and cuSignal handles signal processing.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":205,"question":"You are fine-tuning a sentiment analysis model and notice that the model performs well on positive and negative sentiments but poorly on neutral sentiment. The training data shows that neutral examples make up only 5% of the dataset. Which approach would best address this?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Remove neutral examples to simplify the classification task to binary."},{"key":"b","text":"Apply loss function weighting to give higher importance to neutral class predictions."},{"key":"c","text":"Increase the model size to capture more nuanced patterns."},{"key":"d","text":"Reduce the learning rate to make the model converge more slowly."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Loss function weighting assigns higher penalties for misclassifying underrepresented classes (neutral sentiment), forcing the model to pay more attention to these examples during training. Removing neutral examples eliminates a valid category, larger models don't solve imbalance, and lower learning rates don't address class distribution issues.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":206,"question":"You're training a deep neural network for image classification and notice the model performs significantly better on the training set than the validation set. Which regularization technique would be most effective for addressing this?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Increase the number of layers in the network."},{"key":"b","text":"Apply dropout with a rate of 0.5 to hidden layers."},{"key":"c","text":"Use a larger learning rate to escape local minima."},{"key":"d","text":"Add more training data augmentation without any regularization."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"The performance gap between training and validation indicates overfitting. Dropout with a rate of 0.5 randomly sets half of the hidden layer neurons to zero during training, forcing the network to learn more robust features that generalize better. Adding layers increases complexity (worsening overfitting), larger learning rates cause instability, and augmentation alone without regularization may not sufficiently address the issue.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":207,"question":"You're building an AI-powered application that needs to make asynchronous API calls to multiple language model endpoints. Which Python approach would best handle concurrent requests while managing timeout and error handling?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Use sequential requests with try-except blocks for each call."},{"key":"b","text":"Use asyncio with aiohttp for concurrent API calls with timeout handling."},{"key":"c","text":"Use multiprocessing to spawn separate processes for each API call."},{"key":"d","text":"Use a simple for loop with the requests library and sleep intervals."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"asyncio with aiohttp provides efficient asynchronous I/O-bound concurrency, ideal for making multiple API calls simultaneously without blocking. It handles timeouts and errors gracefully through async/await patterns. Sequential requests are slow, multiprocessing is overkill for I/O-bound tasks, and a for loop with sleep is the least efficient approach.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":208,"question":"You are developing an AI recruitment tool for a large corporation. During testing, you discover the model shows bias against certain demographic groups. Which approach would be most appropriate for ensuring fair AI practices?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Deploy the model as-is and monitor for complaints."},{"key":"b","text":"Remove all demographic information from the training data only."},{"key":"c","text":"Implement bias detection metrics and apply debiasing techniques such as adversarial debiasing, then conduct regular fairness audits."},{"key":"d","text":"Limit the model's use to only demographic groups where it performs well."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A comprehensive approach combining bias detection metrics, debiasing techniques (like adversarial debiasing), and regular fairness audits ensures the model is equitable. Simply removing demographic features may not eliminate proxy bias, deploying biased models is unethical, and limiting use groups does not solve the fundamental issue.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":209,"question":"You are building a data preprocessing pipeline for a large-scale image dataset. The pipeline needs to apply complex transformations (resizing, normalization, augmentation) to millions of images. Which approach would be most efficient?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Process images sequentially using PIL/Pillow on CPU."},{"key":"b","text":"Use GPU-accelerated preprocessing with NVIDIA DALI for parallel image transformations."},{"key":"c","text":"Load all images into memory first, then apply transformations."},{"key":"d","text":"Use basic NumPy operations for all image manipulations."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NVIDIA DALI (Data Loading Library) is specifically designed for GPU-accelerated data preprocessing pipelines, providing parallel image loading and transformation that significantly outperforms CPU-based approaches. Sequential PIL processing is slow, loading everything to memory is impractical for millions of images, and NumPy lacks GPU acceleration for image operations.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":210,"question":"You have deployed an LLM-based customer service chatbot. After 3 months, user satisfaction has dropped due to the model providing outdated information. Which approach would best address this while maintaining model quality?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Retrain the entire model from scratch with updated data every month."},{"key":"b","text":"Implement a continuous fine-tuning pipeline with user feedback and updated knowledge bases."},{"key":"c","text":"Replace the model with a rule-based system for more controlled responses."},{"key":"d","text":"Simply increase the model temperature to generate more diverse responses."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A continuous fine-tuning pipeline incorporating user feedback and updated knowledge bases allows the model to stay current while maintaining quality. Full retraining is expensive and slow, rule-based systems lack the flexibility of LLMs, and increasing temperature adds randomness rather than accuracy.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":211,"question":"You are building a question-answering system that needs to provide accurate answers from a large corporate knowledge base of 100,000+ documents. Which combination of techniques would most effectively enhance your RAG pipeline? (Pick the 2 correct responses)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Use a basic keyword search for document retrieval."},{"key":"b","text":"Fine-tune the embedding model on domain-specific corporate documents."},{"key":"c","text":"Implement a vector database with semantic search for efficient retrieval."},{"key":"d","text":"Rely solely on the LLM's parametric knowledge without retrieval."}],"answers":["b","c"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Fine-tuning the embedding model on domain-specific documents improves retrieval relevance, and using a vector database with semantic search enables efficient similarity-based retrieval from large document collections. Keyword search is less precise for semantic queries, and relying solely on parametric knowledge leads to outdated or hallucinated answers.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":212,"question":"You are building a text classification pipeline for customer reviews. Which combination of preprocessing steps using Python NLP libraries would be most effective?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Use only regex for tokenization and classification."},{"key":"b","text":"Apply spaCy for tokenization and lemmatization, then use scikit-learn's TF-IDF vectorizer for feature extraction."},{"key":"c","text":"Use NLTK for sentiment analysis without any preprocessing."},{"key":"d","text":"Apply basic string splitting and feed raw text directly to the model."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Using spaCy for linguistic preprocessing (tokenization, lemmatization) combined with scikit-learn's TF-IDF vectorizer creates a robust feature extraction pipeline. spaCy handles text normalization while TF-IDF captures term importance. Regex alone is insufficient, skipping preprocessing reduces accuracy, and raw text feeding ignores linguistic structure.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":213,"question":"You are developing a machine learning model to predict customer behavior in an e-commerce platform. The dataset has 200 features. Which approach would be most effective for feature selection to improve model performance?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Use all 200 features to maximize the amount of information."},{"key":"b","text":"Randomly select a subset of features to reduce dimensionality."},{"key":"c","text":"Apply Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE) to identify the most predictive features."},{"key":"d","text":"Use only the first 10 features in the dataset alphabetically."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE) systematically removes less important features by training the model, ranking features by importance, and removing the least important ones iteratively. This produces an optimal subset of predictive features. Using all features risks overfitting, random selection is arbitrary, and alphabetical selection has no statistical basis.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":214,"question":"You are building an AI model for a financial trading platform that needs to make predictions about stock price movements. The model needs to account for uncertainty in its predictions. Which approach would best address this requirement?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Use a simple regression model and report point estimates."},{"key":"b","text":"Add a probabilistic layer to your neural network that outputs prediction distributions rather than single values."},{"key":"c","text":"Round all predictions to the nearest whole number."},{"key":"d","text":"Use ensemble averaging of multiple identical models."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A probabilistic layer outputs prediction distributions (mean and variance) rather than single point estimates, allowing the system to quantify uncertainty. This is crucial for financial applications where understanding prediction confidence informs risk management. Point estimates lack uncertainty information, rounding removes precision, and identical model ensembles don't capture epistemic uncertainty well.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":215,"question":"You are optimizing an LLM for deployment on edge devices with limited computational resources. The current model has 7 billion parameters and needs to achieve sub-100ms latency. Which optimization strategy would be most effective?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Increase the batch size to process more tokens simultaneously."},{"key":"b","text":"Use model distillation to create a smaller model, then apply quantization to reduce precision to INT4/INT8."},{"key":"c","text":"Simply reduce the number of transformer layers without any other optimization."},{"key":"d","text":"Deploy the full model and increase the device's cooling capacity."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Combining model distillation (training a smaller student model from the larger teacher) with quantization (reducing numerical precision to INT4/INT8) provides the most significant size and latency reduction while maintaining acceptable performance. Larger batch sizes increase memory usage, naively removing layers degrades quality, and cooling doesn't reduce computational requirements.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":216,"question":"You have deployed an LLM-based code review tool that occasionally flags correct code as buggy (false positives). Which approach would most effectively reduce these false positives while maintaining detection accuracy?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Increase the model temperature to generate more varied responses."},{"key":"b","text":"Remove the model and use only rule-based static analysis."},{"key":"c","text":"Add a secondary validation layer that cross-references flagged issues with known code patterns and unit test results."},{"key":"d","text":"Simply increase the confidence threshold, ignoring all low-confidence predictions."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A secondary validation layer that cross-references with known patterns and test results adds a verification step that filters false positives while retaining true bugs. Higher temperature increases randomness, pure rule-based systems miss subtle issues, and raising the confidence threshold alone may miss genuine bugs.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":217,"question":"You are building an LLM-powered virtual assistant that needs to maintain context across long multi-turn conversations. The model has a 4096-token context window. Which approach would best handle conversations that exceed this limit?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Simply truncate older messages when the context window is full."},{"key":"b","text":"Implement a sliding window mechanism with summarization of older context to maintain conversation coherence."},{"key":"c","text":"Restart the conversation when the context limit is reached."},{"key":"d","text":"Increase the temperature to compensate for lost context."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A sliding window with summarization condenses older conversation turns into summaries while keeping recent messages in full, maintaining coherence within the limited context window. Simple truncation loses important context, restarting disrupts conversations, and temperature has nothing to do with context management.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":218,"question":"You need to cluster a large collection of unlabeled customer support tickets into meaningful categories. Which approach would be most effective?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Manually read each ticket and assign categories."},{"key":"b","text":"Use regex pattern matching to categorize tickets by keywords."},{"key":"c","text":"Generate text embeddings using a pre-trained model, then apply KMeans clustering to identify natural groupings."},{"key":"d","text":"Sort tickets alphabetically and divide them into equal-sized groups."}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Generating embeddings with a pre-trained model captures semantic meaning of tickets, and KMeans clustering groups semantically similar tickets together without needing labels. Manual categorization doesn't scale, regex misses semantic nuance, and alphabetical sorting has no semantic basis.","topic":"Evaluation & ML Fundamentals"},{"id":219,"question":"You are developing a Named Entity Recognition (NER) system for medical records using spaCy. Which combination of techniques would best improve entity recognition accuracy? (Pick the 2 correct responses)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Use only spaCy's default English model without any customization."},{"key":"b","text":"Apply NumPy's vectorized operations for efficient batch processing of text features."},{"key":"c","text":"Fine-tune spaCy's NER model on a domain-specific medical annotated dataset."},{"key":"d","text":"Use basic string matching for all entity detection."}],"answers":["b","c"],"multi":true,"explanation":"NumPy's vectorized operations enable efficient batch processing of text features at scale, and fine-tuning spaCy's NER on domain-specific annotated medical data teaches the model to recognize specialized medical entities. Default models lack medical domain knowledge, and basic string matching is too rigid for the variability of medical terminology.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":220,"question":"You are developing an AI system that processes personal health records. Which data handling approach would best ensure compliance with privacy regulations while maintaining model effectiveness?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Store all data in plain text for easy access."},{"key":"b","text":"Implement data anonymization techniques, including de-identification of personal information and differential privacy during model training."},{"key":"c","text":"Only collect data from users who provide verbal consent."},{"key":"d","text":"Use data encryption at rest only, without any anonymization."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Data anonymization through de-identification (removing PII) combined with differential privacy during training provides comprehensive privacy protection while allowing the model to learn patterns from the data. Plain text storage is insecure, verbal consent alone is insufficient, and encryption at rest without anonymization still exposes data during processing.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":221,"question":"You are developing a healthcare AI system that needs to make treatment recommendations. Which approach would best ensure the model makes ethical and unbiased recommendations across different patient demographics?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Train the model exclusively on data from the largest demographic group for consistency."},{"key":"b","text":"Build the training dataset to include diverse, representative patient demographics, and implement regular bias audits on the model's outputs."},{"key":"c","text":"Deploy the model without bias testing to get it to market faster."},{"key":"d","text":"Use only clinical trial data, which may not reflect real-world patient diversity."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A diverse, representative training dataset combined with regular bias audits ensures equitable treatment recommendations across demographics. Training on a single group introduces bias, skipping bias testing is irresponsible for healthcare AI, and clinical trial data alone may not reflect real-world diversity.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":222,"question":"You are designing a chatbot for a travel agency that needs to handle dynamic, multi-turn conversations about trip planning. Which architecture would be most suitable for maintaining context and generating coherent responses?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A rule-based chatbot with predefined conversation flows."},{"key":"b","text":"A transformer-based architecture with attention mechanisms for context retention across conversation turns."},{"key":"c","text":"A simple bag-of-words model for intent classification only."},{"key":"d","text":"A recurrent neural network without attention mechanisms."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Transformer-based architectures with attention mechanisms excel at maintaining context across multi-turn conversations by attending to all previous relevant tokens. Rule-based systems lack flexibility, bag-of-words loses word order, and RNNs without attention struggle with long-range dependencies in extended conversations.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":223,"question":"You are building an image generation system using a diffusion model. During the denoising process, the model needs to iteratively refine a noisy image into a coherent output. Which description best captures what happens at each denoising step?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The model adds more noise to explore a wider variety of possible outputs."},{"key":"b","text":"The model predicts and removes a portion of noise, gradually revealing the underlying image structure."},{"key":"c","text":"The model compresses the image to reduce dimensionality before reconstruction."},{"key":"d","text":"The model applies a fixed filter to sharpen the image at each step."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"At each denoising step, the diffusion model predicts the noise component in the current image and removes it, progressively uncovering the image structure. This iterative refinement from pure noise to coherent output is the core of reverse diffusion. Adding noise is the forward process, compression is unrelated, and a fixed filter doesn't adapt to content.","topic":"AI Foundations"},{"id":224,"question":"A startup needs to deploy a multilingual chatbot capable of handling 10+ languages with real-time response requirements. Their budget is limited. Which deployment approach would be most cost-effective while maintaining quality?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Build custom models for each language from scratch."},{"key":"b","text":"Use a cloud-based LLM API with GPU acceleration and a multilingual model, combined with caching for common queries."},{"key":"c","text":"Deploy separate servers for each language in different regions."},{"key":"d","text":"Use a single CPU-based server with a monolingual model."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A cloud-based LLM API with GPU acceleration and a multilingual model provides cost-effective scalability, and caching common queries reduces API calls and latency. Building custom models is expensive, separate servers per language is overengineered, and a CPU monolingual server can't handle the requirements.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":225,"question":"You are building a RAG-based legal document analysis system. The system needs to retrieve relevant legal clauses from a database of contracts. Which approach would most effectively improve retrieval accuracy?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Use generic word embeddings trained on Wikipedia data."},{"key":"b","text":"Fine-tune the embedding model on a corpus of legal documents to capture domain-specific terminology and relationships."},{"key":"c","text":"Use keyword matching with legal terms only."},{"key":"d","text":"Index documents by their creation date for chronological retrieval."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Fine-tuning embeddings on legal documents teaches the model to understand domain-specific terminology, clause structures, and legal relationships, significantly improving retrieval precision. Generic embeddings miss legal nuances, keyword matching is too rigid, and date-based indexing is irrelevant to semantic content.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":226,"question":"You are deploying an LLM for a real-time language translation service in a mobile app. Users in remote areas experience high latency. Which optimization would most effectively reduce response time?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Increase the server's RAM to handle more concurrent requests."},{"key":"b","text":"Deploy a smaller, optimized model on edge computing devices closer to users."},{"key":"c","text":"Switch to a larger model for better translation quality."},{"key":"d","text":"Add more logging to track the source of latency."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Deploying a smaller, optimized model on edge computing devices reduces network latency by processing translations closer to users rather than routing to distant servers. More RAM doesn't reduce network latency, larger models increase processing time, and logging is diagnostic rather than a solution.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":227,"question":"You are building a data preprocessing pipeline for a machine learning model that predicts customer behavior. The dataset contains numerical features (age, income) and categorical features (region, product_category). Which combination of preprocessing steps would be most appropriate? (Pick the 2 correct responses)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Apply standard text tokenization to all features regardless of type."},{"key":"b","text":"Normalize numerical features using Min-Max scaling or StandardScaler."},{"key":"c","text":"Apply one-hot encoding to categorical features (region, product_category)."},{"key":"d","text":"Convert all features to string format for uniformity."}],"answers":["b","c"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Normalizing numerical features ensures they are on comparable scales for model training, and one-hot encoding converts categorical features into binary vectors the model can process. Text tokenization is for NLP data, not structured features, and converting everything to strings loses numerical information.","topic":"NLP & Text Processing"},{"id":228,"question":"What is Generative AI?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A type of AI focused on classifying existing data into categories."},{"key":"b","text":"A type of AI that can create new content, such as text, images, audio, and video, based on patterns learned from training data."},{"key":"c","text":"A type of AI focused on optimizing hardware performance."},{"key":"d","text":"A type of AI that is exclusively used for data analysis and visualization."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Generative AI refers to AI systems capable of creating new content (text, images, audio, video, code) by learning patterns and structures from training data. Unlike discriminative models that classify or predict, generative models produce novel outputs. It is not limited to classification, hardware optimization, or data visualization.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":229,"question":"Why do decoder-only architectures like GPT use causal (masked) self-attention?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To compress the KV cache memory footprint during inference by quantizing stored key-value pairs to lower precision formats like INT8 or FP8"},{"key":"b","text":"To reduce the computational complexity of the self-attention mechanism by approximately half, since only the lower triangular portion of the attention matrix needs to be calculated"},{"key":"c","text":"To enable bidirectional attention patterns where each token can attend to both preceding and following positions, improving contextual understanding of the full sequence"},{"key":"d","text":"To prevent the model from attending to future tokens during training, ensuring each position only depends on previous positions"}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Causal masking blocks attention to positions that come later in the sequence. This lets the model learn next-token prediction without seeing the answer tokens it is supposed to predict.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":230,"question":"In the attention mechanism, what mathematical operation is performed on the Query (Q) and Key (K) matrices to compute attention scores?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Element-wise multiplication of query and key vectors followed by summation across the feature dimension to produce position-wise similarity scores"},{"key":"b","text":"A convolution operation applied across query and key matrices to capture local positional patterns and short-range dependencies between tokens"},{"key":"c","text":"Concatenation of the query and key vectors for each position pair followed by a learned linear transformation to compute compatibility scores"},{"key":"d","text":"Matrix multiplication (dot product) followed by scaling and softmax"}],"answers":["d"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Attention scores begin with the query–key dot product, scaled by the key dimension and passed through softmax to obtain attention weights.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":231,"question":"In language model decoding, what effect does the temperature parameter have on the softmax function during token sampling?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Temperature determines the maximum sequence length the model can generate by scaling the positional encoding boundaries during inference"},{"key":"b","text":"Temperature controls the learning rate during model training, dynamically adjusting the step size based on output confidence scores"},{"key":"c","text":"Higher temperature values make the probability distribution more uniform, increasing output diversity"},{"key":"d","text":"Lower temperature values increase the randomness of generated text by flattening the probability distribution across all vocabulary tokens"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Temperature rescales logits before softmax. A higher temperature flattens the distribution and makes lower-probability tokens more likely; a lower temperature makes sampling more concentrated.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":232,"question":"What is the key difference between the encoder and decoder components in an encoder-decoder transformer architecture?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The encoder exclusively uses self-attention while the decoder relies solely on cross-attention to encoder outputs for all token interactions"},{"key":"b","text":"The decoder uses masked self-attention to prevent attending to future tokens during training, while the encoder uses bidirectional attention"},{"key":"c","text":"The decoder uses larger embedding dimensions and wider feed-forward layers than the encoder to improve generation quality and fluency"},{"key":"d","text":"The encoder typically has more layers than the decoder to better process and encode input representations before passing them to generation"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"The encoder uses bidirectional self-attention over the input. The decoder uses causal (masked) self-attention for generated tokens and typically cross-attention to the encoder output.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":233,"question":"What happens during the token generation process when a decoder-only LLM produces an output?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The model generates all tokens in the output sequence simultaneously in a single forward pass using parallel decoding, producing the complete response at once rather than sequentially"},{"key":"b","text":"The model outputs dense embedding vectors from its final transformer layer that are directly used as the text representation without any additional projection or sampling step"},{"key":"c","text":"The model outputs logits (raw scores) for each vocabulary token, which are converted to probabilities via softmax and then sampled or selected"},{"key":"d","text":"The model directly outputs text characters one at a time using a character-level mapping function, bypassing any intermediate probability computation or vocabulary-based token selection"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"At each generation step, the model produces vocabulary logits. Softmax converts them to probabilities, after which a decoding strategy selects or samples the next token.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":234,"question":"What is Multi-Query Attention (MQA) and how does it differ from standard multi-head attention? (Select TWO)","options":[{"key":"a","text":"MQA uses a single set of key and value projection matrices shared across all attention heads, while maintaining separate query projections per head"},{"key":"b","text":"MQA processes multiple user queries simultaneously in a single forward pass by parallelizing the attention computation across different input sequences in the batch"},{"key":"c","text":"MQA eliminates the query projection matrices entirely and computes attention scores using only the key and value representations, reducing the total parameter count per layer"},{"key":"d","text":"MQA increases the number of attention heads beyond the standard configuration to improve the model's representational capacity and pattern recognition ability"},{"key":"e","text":"MQA significantly reduces the KV cache memory footprint during inference, enabling higher throughput and longer context lengths on the same hardware"}],"answers":["a","e"],"multi":true,"explanation":"Multi-Query Attention keeps separate query projections but shares one key projection and one value projection across heads. This reduces KV-cache storage and can improve inference throughput.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":235,"question":"What is a key advantage of using NVIDIA NeMo for fine-tuning a pre-trained LLM compared to starting training from scratch?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"It requires no labeled data"},{"key":"b","text":"It is faster and computationally less expensive"},{"key":"c","text":"It guarantees better model accuracy"},{"key":"d","text":"It eliminates the need for GPUs"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Fine-tuning starts from an existing model and updates it for a narrower task, generally requiring less data, compute, and time than pretraining a model from scratch.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":236,"question":"Which of the following NVIDIA tools is best suited for optimizing LLM inference performance through precision reduction and layer fusion?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NVIDIA NeMo"},{"key":"b","text":"TensorRT-LLM"},{"key":"c","text":"Triton Inference Server"},{"key":"d","text":"NVIDIA AI Enterprise"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"TensorRT-LLM provides LLM-specific inference optimizations, including low-precision execution and graph/layer optimizations, to improve serving performance.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":237,"question":"How does NVIDIA AI Enterprise facilitate the deployment of generative AI models in an enterprise environment?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"By providing pre-trained models only"},{"key":"b","text":"By offering a scalable and secure AI infrastructure"},{"key":"c","text":"By focusing solely on model training"},{"key":"d","text":"By eliminating the need for GPU hardware"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NVIDIA AI Enterprise provides an enterprise-oriented software stack and infrastructure for developing and deploying AI workloads with operational and security support.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":238,"question":"How does prompt engineering benefit LLM deployment?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"By reducing model size"},{"key":"b","text":"By improving inference speed"},{"key":"c","text":"By enhancing model output relevance"},{"key":"d","text":"By lowering computational cost"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Prompt engineering clarifies the task, constraints, and context supplied to the model. This can make responses more relevant without changing the model weights.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"},{"id":239,"question":"In a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) system, which NVIDIA tool can enhance the efficiency of embedding retrieval?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"TensorRT-LLM"},{"key":"b","text":"NVIDIA NeMo"},{"key":"c","text":"Triton Inference Server"},{"key":"d","text":"NGC Catalog"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NVIDIA NeMo Retriever components support document embedding and retrieval workflows used to find relevant context for RAG applications.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":240,"question":"What is a key advantage of using NVIDIA AI Enterprise for LLM deployment?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"It is open-source"},{"key":"b","text":"It offers seamless integration with NVIDIA hardware and software"},{"key":"c","text":"It provides free cloud resources"},{"key":"d","text":"It automatically writes code"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NVIDIA AI Enterprise is designed to work as an integrated software stack with NVIDIA GPUs and related NVIDIA software, simplifying supported enterprise AI deployment.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":241,"question":"Which strategy would be most effective in reducing the latency of a deployed LLM using NVIDIA Triton Inference Server?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Increasing the batch size"},{"key":"b","text":"Using mixed precision inference"},{"key":"c","text":"Deploying on a single GPU"},{"key":"d","text":"Enabling model ensemble features"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Mixed-precision inference can use lower-precision arithmetic where supported, reducing memory traffic and improving GPU throughput while retaining suitable output quality.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":242,"question":"What is a key consideration when implementing retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) using NVIDIA tools for a document summarization task?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Ensuring the model is trained solely on retrieval data"},{"key":"b","text":"Optimizing the context window size to balance retrieval accuracy and model performance"},{"key":"c","text":"Using only pre-trained models without any fine-tuning"},{"key":"d","text":"Focusing exclusively on increasing the number of retrieved documents"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"The retrieved context must fit the model context window. Choosing an appropriate context size balances relevant evidence against latency, memory use, and the risk of adding noisy passages.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"RAG & Knowledge Grounding"},{"id":243,"question":"In which scenario would you use NVIDIA NeMo's pre-trained models as a starting point?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Developing a custom chatbot from scratch"},{"key":"b","text":"Optimizing inference with TensorRT"},{"key":"c","text":"Fine-tuning a model for domain-specific language understanding"},{"key":"d","text":"Deploying models using NVIDIA Triton"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A pre-trained NeMo model is a useful starting point when adapting language understanding to a domain: fine-tuning can specialize its learned representations for domain vocabulary and tasks.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":244,"question":"Which NVIDIA tool would you use to deploy a multimodal generative AI application that requires both text and image processing capabilities?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NVIDIA NeMo"},{"key":"b","text":"TensorRT-LLM"},{"key":"c","text":"NVIDIA Triton Inference Server"},{"key":"d","text":"NVIDIA AI Enterprise"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Triton Inference Server is designed to serve trained models in production, including different model frameworks and modalities. NeMo is primarily used to build and train models; TensorRT-LLM focuses on LLM inference optimization.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":245,"question":"What is the primary advantage of using NVIDIA NeMo for training large language models (LLMs) over traditional frameworks?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NeMo supports mixed precision training, which reduces memory usage and increases training speed."},{"key":"b","text":"NeMo provides a built-in feature for automatic prompt engineering."},{"key":"c","text":"NeMo is optimized for real-time inference only, not training."},{"key":"d","text":"NeMo includes a proprietary tokenization algorithm that improves model accuracy."}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Mixed-precision training uses formats such as FP16 or BF16 for suitable operations to reduce memory consumption and accelerate computation on supported NVIDIA GPUs.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":246,"question":"In the context of deploying LLMs, what is a primary concern when using prompt injection prevention techniques?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Maintaining high throughput"},{"key":"b","text":"Ensuring model interpretability"},{"key":"c","text":"Preventing malicious or unintended inputs"},{"key":"d","text":"Maximizing model accuracy"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Prompt-injection defenses aim to prevent malicious or unintended instructions in user input or retrieved content from overriding the intended system behavior.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":247,"question":"In NVIDIA's AI Enterprise suite, which component specifically facilitates the deployment of AI models across enterprise environments?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NGC Catalog"},{"key":"b","text":"DGX Systems"},{"key":"c","text":"AI Workflows"},{"key":"d","text":"Triton Inference Server"}],"answers":["c"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NVIDIA AI Workflows provide packaged, validated reference workflows and deployment components that help organizations deploy AI solutions across enterprise environments.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"NVIDIA Deployment & Tools"},{"id":248,"question":"When using NVIDIA TensorRT-LLM, what is a common strategy to improve the throughput of a generative AI model?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Increasing batch size"},{"key":"b","text":"Reducing the number of layers"},{"key":"c","text":"Switching to a different model architecture"},{"key":"d","text":"Using more training data"}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"Increasing the effective batch size can improve GPU utilization and throughput in TensorRT-LLM serving, subject to available memory and the latency requirements of the application.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"GPU Computing & Performance"},{"id":249,"question":"How does NVIDIA NeMo support ethical AI development in generative models?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"By providing pre-trained models that are bias-free."},{"key":"b","text":"By offering tools for customizing and implementing ethical guidelines."},{"key":"c","text":"By ensuring models are trained only on curated datasets."},{"key":"d","text":"By limiting the model size to prevent overfitting."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"NeMo Guardrails and related customization mechanisms let developers define and enforce application-specific safety and policy constraints. Pre-trained models are not automatically bias-free.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":250,"question":"In a real-world application using NVIDIA NeMo for document summarization, which strategy would help ensure ethical AI practices are followed?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Utilizing a diverse training dataset"},{"key":"b","text":"Maximizing model size for accuracy"},{"key":"c","text":"Focusing solely on technical documents"},{"key":"d","text":"Increasing the number of model layers"}],"answers":["a"],"multi":false,"explanation":"A diverse and representative dataset helps expose the model to a wider range of language and document styles, reducing the risk that summarization quality is systematically poor for underrepresented sources.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":251,"question":"When using NVIDIA's RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) implementation, what is a primary consideration to ensure ethical AI and responsible development?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Maximize the context window size to include more information."},{"key":"b","text":"Ensure the vector database is regularly updated with vetted, unbiased data."},{"key":"c","text":"Use the largest available embedding model for better accuracy."},{"key":"d","text":"Focus on optimizing the retrieval speed over data quality."}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"RAG quality and safety depend on the evidence being retrieved. Regularly reviewing and updating the vector database with vetted, balanced sources helps reduce stale or biased grounding material.","tip":"Exam takeaway: distinguish the component’s actual role from nearby concepts such as training, inference, serving, and retrieval.","topic":"Responsible AI & Security"},{"id":252,"question":"What is the primary advantage of using QLoRA over traditional LoRA for fine-tuning large language models?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Higher accuracy"},{"key":"b","text":"Reduced computational cost"},{"key":"c","text":"Improved scalability"},{"key":"d","text":"Faster convergence"}],"answers":["b"],"multi":false,"explanation":"QLoRA quantizes the frozen base model (commonly to 4-bit) and trains small low-rank adapters. This substantially lowers the memory needed for fine-tuning, making it practical with more limited hardware resources.","tip":"Exam takeaway: LoRA trains low-rank adapters; QLoRA combines adapters with quantization to reduce the memory footprint further.","topic":"Transformers & Language Models"}];
